        <Document ID="1C95B670-73DB-43F4-ACAB-6B47FE7740E9">
            <Title>(Instructor) All in a vacuum</Title>
            <Text>“Well! First thing first, back to where we began” the teacher insisted politely. “This morning we will be carrying out a deep space airlock exercise. I hope you’re all prepared for the rapid decompression of the chamber.” At a sudden instance all the bulk head doors to the class room closed tight, dead bolting into the ships frame creating an airtight seal. 
“Please note! Any loose items are to be declared now, unless you want it coming back as a missile and putting a hole in you or your friend a few years later” A silent tension overwhelmed the room, the students sat waiting in silent anticipation. One of the students intervened for a moment, “Are these methods not unorthodox, how are we supposed to learn if we are stressed”, “Well” Responded the teacher, “If you cannot operate under stress, then how can you be expected to operate in the real world when things go sideways?” A sudden prang was heard followed by a sharp hissing sound. Air escaped the room at a rapid rate. The students felt their ear drums lightly expand as the room pressure dropped slowly with increasing intensity. 
 In that instant an alarm sounded through the room. “Failure to deploy oxygen masks, please resort to manual donning of masks.” At this moment, the teacher had quickly taken a seat and gone into a meditative state, he did not breathe, but observed silently as the students around him scattered.
One of Shai’s personal paper notes was sucked straight from her uniform pocket fluttering rapidly to the vacuum hole being succeed out into space through a tiny mechanical hole. 
The moment one of the students passed out on the ground, he rose from his seated position, grabbed one of the manual oxygen donning masks and placed it over the student’s mouth. At this time all the students have watched the teacher, grabbed the oxygen masks in the roof holds and placed them around their heads taking in deep breaths of relief. At this moment the air lock has re-engaged and the hole had been mechanically re-sealed. The engineering and thought that had gone into the mechanism was apparent. 
The teacher, without even needing to don a mask, rose to his feet almost as if nothing had occurred. “Now how many of you believed in this instant you were going to Die?” with this the room fell silent. Slowly one by one all of the hands began to raise. All except one at the end of the table; John. He hadn’t raised his hand almost as if to draw attention to himself. John had observed quickly when the airlock initially failed, he noted above him a small latch where the oxygen mask had been contained. He pulled down the latch where an oxygen kit appeared. He placed the first kit on himself, then continued to grab another and assist his partner Tau, regulating her intake to compensate with the vacuum. 
Satisfied with the results and the students, the teacher gave an affirming nod, as if to say “Well you have all passed this test” with that he continued. “It is important that we are able to work under stress, that we collect ourselves. To remain calm in any and all emergencies, the latter is death. If we cannot do this then we will not survive. That is why! This training will push you to your extremes, so that you are ready for the event before it even occurs. We are here to prepare you, we are here to ensure you survive, to ensure you are competent and have the both the physical and technical skills to thrive calmly in such a way that you may have time to assist your shipmates should they lose their way”
&quot;Now!&quot; said the instructor. “I will have your captain take the floor to introduce you to your new suits; I am sure, as you all know, you are in capable hands.” John bowed his head to the instructor ever so lightly, then rose to his feet and began walking decisively around the horseshoe table and his colleagues toward the instructor's position before the class at the other end of the room.</Text>
            <Comments>The teacher should have a character profile </Comments>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="1EE1F653-CF30-4248-BE39-047D356D0FCB">
            <Title>Europa</Title>
            <Text>



The metallic clang of a transport pod coupling with the airlock bay shattered the mess's still silent hum. The sound reverberated through the hull of the Ignotius, dissipating as soon as they had arrived. Ripples atop John’s lifeless coffee faded away. 

“The doctors’ transport pod” Kitten announced breaking the silence. “he just arrived from his station on the surface”. 

John’s senses sharpened. Across the table, Tex stopped spinning his fork, setting it down beside his spotless plate, which he’d meticulously licked clean. Footsteps echoed from the corridor—familiar in a way that tugged at a buried memory within John. His pulse quickened. He had heard those footsteps before, in a dream, just before the briefing on the lost facility. But this time, there were two sets: one heavy, deliberate, and the other lighter, more cautious.

The Ignotius drifted in the void like a giant squid, silent, as the crew exchanged uneasy glances, waiting for what was to come. 
“Doctor.. are these the Protectant you spoke of”, The confused stares of John and his team dissipated as another sentient companion Doc into the room. However, something about this Sentient was different. Tex could only just put his finger on it. “Is it just me or does this thing actually have hips?” He thought to himself.
“I have prepared an overview of the drilling superstructure embedded beneath the ice on Europa” Serra stepped forward emerging from the bulkheads behind Wolfgang, her shoulder sat proudly next to the Doctors, and eyed Tex directly with a soft conservative smile. 
&quot;Serra designed the facility herself: She ... was a board member on the Europa exploration team. head architect.&quot; The Doctor gave praise in his statement. &quot;We worked together to test the viability of the worker bots in the high radiation of Europa's surface.&quot; Serra stepped forward she could tell Tex was the most uncomfortable about her sudden emergence into the room, although she put it down to sexual tension internally, he was just shy and she saw straight through it. &quot;As well as pressure testing for the depth. My role was crucial in designing the hyperbaric chambers in a way that could convert the hydrogen to cells for the worker bot program” The Doctor announced “Nova was extraordinary in her field!” with a tone of excitement, that filled Tau with joy, Tau really liked her, and you could see it in the way she rested a keen eye across her visor flickering with eagerness as she spoke. 
To Shai Serra invoked a sense of a lost kindred mother, hidden beneath the wires. She found it hard to fathom the kind of knowledge she would have accessed this past 150 years. Shai leant over to Tau perching respectfully behind her ear and in an instant whispered softly “I like this one”. Tau met her comment with a smile, Shai immediately spotted her dimples and met with a cute contained giggle. 
“I am sure you and Shai will get along just fine”, Doc drew his attention from Serra to Shai, gesturing at Shai with his human like hand. “Shai is our Deep Space Medic Technician, if something goes wrong medically work with her in any extractions that may occur”. Serra’s template emoted what looked akin to a smile. “You must update me on the progress of the genetic tree printing bio-gel after this briefing Shai, I would be interested in your abstract around Neural flexibility in this new field”. Tex raised his eyebrows, with a slightly impressed tone but shying on impatience. “I am sure you will have plenty of time to get acquitted after the mission brief”. John gently redirected the attention of the room while nodding respectfully at the Doctor. 



From behind the Doctor

</Text>
