        <Document ID="58A6D374-C237-47B0-97F0-732F8DA1E4A9">
            <Title>Am I dreaming or am i dead</Title>
            <Text>Ringing pierced Tex’s ears with a strange bubbling static. It wasn’t a normal ringing but an alien tinnitus he had never felt before. It was as though he had fallen into a pool, but without the press of the waters pressure against him. Sound felt weightless, in the sense that he could not feel percussion, it was just there, untouchable, but present. 
 
It was like his ears were being born into existence for the first time, hearing the first sounds he had ever heard. There was something entirely off. Like he was no longer a part of his own body, or any body. 

All he could see was white emptiness. Eventually the ringing stopped, There was no taste, smell or feeling of weight. He felt senseless, free of the body, but he still had a conscience. He could still feel the pain brought on by memories, all his past trauma, but only in the mind. He had no breath or pulse. He drifted in the minds construct, and in this while he felt in control, he could not help but feel like he was at the complete mercy of whatever this was. He embraced the silence, trusting the process. It was the first peaceful moment he had felt in what seemed like an eternity. Nothing could harm him here other than his thoughts, his very being was that of his own will. 

Then the silence stopped. He could feel again. He was falling, battling against a cold piercing wind that stormed against his face.  All sense of feeling came back to him, right until before his body floated ever so slowly through the roof of a small  seemingly insignificant container secluded, full and univiting. This container while in his mind he knew it seemed inconvenient, he was revisiting an old place he was all too familiar with. The pilot containers of the cleanup crew called in after the war. He remembered everything as though he had just breathed the cold air, and flet hte mist of his breath fog the screen which emmitted artificial light thorugh  the dark void surrounding him.  he recalled the feeling of the cold joystick when he grasped it, strangely no matter how long he held onto that joystick controlling the machines, scraping the remnants of a history and culture he could only grasp at in a distant lifetime. No longer how long he held the joy-stick, it was cold and heavy. It didn’t matter how much debris he cleared from that container, He could not help but feel like he was berrying a part of himself in that strange foreign land. And while the news spoke of remembrance, it was he who bared the images, the burnt flesh, the artifacts and cherrished belongings, berried deep beneath a soil now only able to bear not life, but the archives of a generation he would never know. In his sleep they came back to him, for many years to come. He wandered on in his dreams for what felt like eternity. When he woke he would read poetry, wonder if his children would feel the weight on his breath, the strangling fold of mankinds injustice. 

 . His stomach, or whatever he flet of it was all but gone. What lifht would shine through the small window of htat container was always jaded by an overcast cloud. Although the cloud was not real, it was a cloud of doubt in the mission which h ewas fighting for. The boards beneath him felt thin. He like he could fall through at any moment. 


 a cold wind 

I have been dreaming allot lately. Last night I dreamt of her, my x. I feel sad to think of her. The dream felt convicting, as we walked into a great hall, but this hall was different to a normal court. It was an open area and instead of rows. The judge sat atop a podium looking down over all those who entered in single file along a white proud and gloss coated fence. The white fence gleaned and slithered it’s way around green well kept greens of medium length grass, soft to the touch, forgiving more than the onwatchers to those who lost balance and fell. The whole room felt like a balancing act where those around you watched on as you tried to keep balance while paying attention to the front. It felt likeby holding hands you may become more interlinked in this position. I kept losing my balance, once I had found my place. The sad part in this passage is that at one point when I felt I had found my place, we were all looking around at one another. Below the Judges stand, there was a row looking back at us, stood atop their own white fence with smooth rails.. It was in this moment, when I made eye contact with her,  that I felt just how awkward the rail of teh fence I was standing on felt. The rail was not flat so that one could keep balance with ease. It was curved, depriving the heel any grounding you perched atop on your souls finding balance on you toes. If you leant back to far on your heels you would soon meet the ground below you. While I made contact with her, she was smiling, not looking at me. It was as though I could see her but I was completely invisible to her. The person next to her smiled aswell, as they exchanged glances between one another. I wanted to be him, smiling with her. I felt my balance begin to tip, and eventually I fell back, ripping the rail off with me. I was confused to find the rail had been attached by nails instead of screws. Perhaps if it had been fastened with screws, It may have caught my fall as I reached with my feet underneath it to correct my balance. But it only gave way. I felt conflicted between embarrassment and frustration. Once I found my position again. I found her again with my eyes, still she hadn’t noticed me. I edged closer along the rails, trying to get a closer glance at her. As I got closer I slowly made out her jacket, a dark navy blue with a dark glossed emblem faint by the angle but the light that licked it pronounced the emblemes distinct features. She was wearing a police sweater, and for some reason this made me happy, like she had found a place among a group, but I felt even further away from her not being a part of it. She had moved on with a new life without me. And the longer I waited the further awayay she went. I don’t know why I feel so dead inside after this dream. Perhaps it si me still trying to let go I have a partner now, times have been uncertain for us. Perhaps it is in the times of uncertainty we often look back on past relationships at what once felt grounded. Admittedly Mya was a very grounded person, one of her strenghts was her persistance and stubbornness to fight not only for herself but her family back home. This amounted an unwavering dedication within her, that surpassed any urge to give up when confronted with 3rd world issues like getting along with her peers. The terrible strength that suffering bestows upon people. I feel guilty in a sense like I abandoned her. I did abandon her. Thats hard to live with at times . And makes it even more convicting. The open court of pasture I feel is a place where I am happy to be in commonality with others, but a part of it is confronting. It is symbolic of how far we have come and are yet to come in our shared capacity. But the uncertainty in all the beauty comes from our struggle to find balance within ourselves. The nails feel deliberate in thes sense that we must not rely on our footing to hold us up, but our own feet instead. It is not just our body but our mind that keeps the balance, if one should fail, we cannot expect the ground beneath us to save us, but instead find grounding in our mind first.  





   </Text>
            <Comments>ASUS, 28/04/2026 6:35 AM
