        <Document ID="73E82DD1-5A03-4910-8E01-215A2C2FCD73">
            <Title>Checking the saddle</Title>
            <Text>Checking the Saddle. 
The ship itself was much smaller than its holding bay, out of place. The scaled of the bay suggested it had been designed for something much, much larger. The sheer enormity of the structure detracted from the little ship's aesthetic and design; it felt insignificant in comparison. John walked slowly up to the underbelly of the vessel, prying closely at its hull, observing the alien insignia etched into its side. A blanket of dust obscured its view from the others, as John, in direct line of sight, his inbuilt computer began trying to translate although struggled, splurting out only fragments at a time. Curiosity gripped him as he raised his palm to its belly. He seemed to approach the ship with caution like it was alive, more than just a shell of cold steel enshrouding technology buried deep within the mountain but an exoskeleton protecting organs its creators had so delicately pieced together.
As confidence rose within him, he pressed his palm flat on its underbelly then wiped away the dust that once obscured the insignia in two smooth firm sweeps. John's kitten had compared encrypted data banks from files buried deep in the lost facilities archives translating the strange language. John skipped a breath in thought for a moment, taken aback by the now revealed name, then chuckled, &quot;Well, that's a little weird!&quot;
Tau was silently captivated, trying to piece together in her mind how the thrusters' careful placement around the ship would fly her. Vector lines formed around its shell outward in her mind like pen to pad. &quot;Huh,&quot; said Tau. &quot;Did you say something, Cap?&quot;
&quot;The name of the ship, at least that's what I think it is... it's the same as my childhood horse on the farm I grew up on.&quot;
Tau wandered over to John's side, looking up at the insignia with him. &quot;I can't make it out... B... beve...&quot; Tau lipped at the insignia like a confused child.
&quot;Here,&quot; said John, flicking her the lost facilities files from his wrist terminal. &quot;See if that helps.&quot; Slowly her HUD began to translate the insignia, the word slowly came out of her lips as each letter turned over. &quot;Beverly...&quot; its edges kissed by the dusty blanket of time. &quot;Beautiful name, though strange for curation from something made in a lab,&quot; Tau concluded.
&quot;It makes you wonder how pure sentients' personalities are formed, from what memories? What made them who they are to begin with, do they have set precursors with selected memories added on top, or just pure akin programming from the start?&quot;
Shai was scanning over the medical equipment and rations in the trip supply brought over by Tebo. &quot;Would you happen to have an inventory file, my metal friend?&quot; said Shai.
&quot;Sure, lassy.&quot; Tebo's mechanical legs sprang with a joyous formality at her request.
&quot;I've just sent you over the inventory data,&quot; said John. Tebo had linked with a mere thought, the file paths to their scheduled supply inventory. He wandered over to Shai leaning in softly like a warm smile, his hands on his hips.
&quot;You're all in for a long flight ahead. Once you make it past the orbital blockade, you humies will need all the nutrients you can get while you sleep through your high-g burn.&quot; A supply crate hovered no more than half an inch above metal tracks that had been recessed into the slab of the ship's bay, one leading to each of the bays that stretched on for about three football fields.
The supply crate opened out, presenting several compartments, one with medical supplies, another loaded with a variety of fruits, meat, poultry, as well as canisters loaded with nutrient-rich liquids that, premade, looked as though they had been designed like cartridges. Each labeled with the strange insignia that could be seen throughout the sentient hold.
&quot;I'm beginning to like these guys,&quot; muffled Tex, as he helped himself to an apple from the produce compartment with yet another crisp chomp. &quot;Damn... these are good!&quot; he proceeded, chewing through it like a wild malnourished horse that had just survived the winter.
&quot;First the ship name, now horse chops over, I am really getting taken back to the paddock,&quot; said John.
&quot;Maybe it's a sign you should get back in that saddle, cowboy,&quot; said Tau, resting her hand gently on John's shoulder with a slight eased shake. She looked on through the rear cargo hold of Beverly to the bridge. She nodded her head at the silhouette of something John felt all too familiar with, letting out a soft smile like she knew deep down, John would be in a state of nostalgia if he just lingered a little while further inside. The hangar light dimly illuminated the outline of a black object, reaching farther down into the cargo hold of the ship to catch John’s eye. The pilot’s seat of the Beverly was constructed of a pristine black leather. It sat there silently, alluring, untouched evident by the specs of dust that gently lined it’s contours. John looked confused for a moment, then slowly made his way up through the beverly’s belly to the guided like a firefly toward the seat. John rested his hand on the shoulder hilt of the chair, running it down it’s side, as though caressing with his palm. It seemed like it had filled a void in his heart he had forgotten about. 
“This doesn’t look like a ship built by the sentient” His voice was riddled with question as he became lost in the relic from the past, that laid now quietly in a womb of technological feat beyond it’s original birthplace. He felt himself being drawn deeper to it, comforted by it’s hold. It felt familiar, although he had never flown such a ship, at least he thought that he hadn’t as far back as he could remember. 
The outside of the Beverley was all but smooth, with fine contours like a stealth bird. Defences weren't entirely apparent, which was a little concerning for a team about to slip through an orbital defense barrage. Yet the inside of the Beverley was nostalgic for John, as if he had seen it before in some distant dream.
The cockpit of the Beverley was small—so small it humbled you, especially for a squad of Protectants who had just come from living aboard a ship like the Balaenoptera with its spacious hallways and open-design bridge.
&quot;Why does this feel like something from Back to the Future?&quot; Shai had just eased her head into the cockpit from the cargo hold. &quot;Don't press the red button.&quot; Tam smiled back at Shai, looking over her shoulder. She had already removed one of the panels under the cockpit dash and was mapping out her control system.
&quot;All good on inventory,&quot; said John. &quot;All good, Cap!&quot; Shai shot back. &quot;Good work.&quot; John slapped Shai on the shoulder with a friendly squeeze, then made his way back out to where Tex was still stuffing his face, familiarising himself with the point defense cannons along the Beverley's belly.
&quot;How is our firing arc PDCs?&quot; asked John. &quot;Any blind spots?&quot;. Tex was crouched over, actuating a switch under the main control panel. &quot;Manual checks showing full range of motion on the gyros,&quot; he said, wiping some grease from his forehead and sweating profusely. &quot;The stern port side PDC Y-axis has a limitation at 170°, slight kink, but we can make up for it with the bow port cannon. We are high-tailing it anyway. Unless you plan on paying Marcellus some return love from his theatrics yesterday, we should be fine.&quot;
Tex was looking John in the eye with a serious fixture. His eyes screamed vengeance, like he had taken a king hit to the back of the head, and Marcellus was behind it.
“Good work Tex” Said John, dismissing his last comment as a suicide run and shear action on impulse. “High tailing it… I get it” Sighed Tex. Ducking his head back under the PCD’s control panel. “Send me the updated defence sector coverage when you are done!” Said John, making his way over to the loading bay where Tebo was tinkering away. Tebo’s hand was nothing like a hand a usual human hand, despite the obvious design of the sentient to sync with the human neural network. A small probe retracted back forearm, the final component tucked neatly into his index finger seamlessly .John looked at this with amazement and slight concern, then yelled back to Tex still berried deep underneath panels along the internal hull of the ship. “I need to know which soft spots we have so I can turn them away from any heat we might have coming our way!”
Tex didn’t respond initially but he heard the captain nonetheless. His focus acutely honed in on bringing the mid ship’s PDC into an acceptable motion range. “Cap these aren’t the original fittings of the ship.. They’re updates. I don’t think this thing was intended as a gun ship. It’s just not fitted with enough guts to support your typical PDC, I mean unless your firing snow peas at the enemy I don’t know what they were expecting mounting these here”. 
Tebo walked over next to John, “Precisely, we didn’t need to reinforce her. The PDC’s we designed are purely for defence purposes. They aren’t meant to breach the armour of typical fleet’s… just ordinance”.. John was silent contemplating what they were getting themselves into. Tex paused his inspection abruptly pulling himself out from under the gunnel where the PDC panels laid. “You mean to tell me we are supposed to intimidate anyone coming at us head on with harsh language!” chuckled Tex. “Aaah heck well I’m your guy, just make sure you have a secure channel to their bridge on max volume before we enter the fight, and I’ll make sure they’re ears are ringing with sweet melodies” Tex stood eyeing off the vague visor of tebo, the usual scattered chatter accross his dome showed a lack of thought. He couldn’t tell if he was being serious or joking. 
“If all else fails my friend we do have other measures” Tebo exclaimed. “Lets hope we don’t have to use them. The aim is to slip past the hole without waking the fox”. John paused for a moment looking back to the little android man, “Right Tebo..” Said John, “Theoretically yes”. said Tebo with no emotionin his response. The lack there of strangely instilled John with a silent confidence in his plan. Afterall, they had survived undetected over half a century. He couldn’t fault his logic, whatever it was. He trusted him.  



</Text>
            <Comments>CONTINUITY SPELLING: Suggested canonical ship name = Beverly. If you prefer Beverley, standardize every ship reference and the worldbuilding entry to that spelling.
CONTINUITY SPELLING: Suggested canonical ship name = Beverly. If you prefer Beverley, standardize every ship reference and the worldbuilding entry to that spelling.</Comments>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="74470BCA-713D-4869-82FD-8C4E130D9896">
            <Title>Chapter 3: Synaptic Memoriam of a steel fossil shell</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="74EBBDBB-5ECF-44E3-BE8F-A369933490FC">
            <Title>(John) Say hello to the Mark IV</Title>
            <Text>“Thanks, Doc,” said John, turning to face the students. “Some of you,” John began, “will be effectively donning these new suits in less than 24 hours. So you will need your ears, your eyes, your noses, your conscience and your team,” John paused, then continued in a softer tone, “so some of you will need to be here in this room. Please let me know if I am boring you, and I will improvise to meet your style.”
John’s eyes rested warmly on the rest of the class, with his left arm down at his side, his right lifted to his chest center, fingers gently pinched with his expression. The Captain exerted a humble confidence in his soft voice. He sounded rested despite the early wake-up, there was a clarity in the way he spoke, the words flowed out of him like a poetry he had recited earlier.  
“In 15 hours we will be powering up these suits for our last Earth deployment before departure to Europa. This is the sentry suit’s first active trial” John’s voice was confident, but the class could sense he was slightly uneased by the short notice he had been given. 
“John, we haven't trained for a halo jump in over a year now!” Tex sighed as he deflated in his chair, stern and concerned, looking directly at john. “We’ve got some sleep implant simulations for the new Halo drop program” John’s reassurance fell on what seemed deaf ears, Tex leaning back on his chair, his arms crossed in deflection. “You’ll have the waking 2 hours before 0600 to get it right, and you’ll still be asleep, effectively you should wake up trained, as opposed to a half day planning for a 30 minute jump, you’ll get four hours to warm up. A quarter of the time it usually takes with real preparations. Call that intensified learning”.
Tex tilted his head in convincing approval, his tower-like shoulders shrugging as he took up two seat bays. He looked directly at John “Anything to keep up with the times. Mind to the machine, got us training in our sleep now Cap.”
John nodded at Tex, his arms folded across his chest, then averted his eyes to the rest of the class, “Try and get in as many simulations as you can, You all have the image of the gear in your head now, stick to the basics, the suit’s well centered. keeps your kits spread balanced, but you will still need to do your personal checks for your own pack which will go in the suit’s hold” 
John paused for a moment, then subtly leaned his direction towards Shai “We don’t want a barrel roll show like last time” John winked at Shai discreetly but continued walking by as if nothing had occurred. John then rubbed his eye to conceal it, as though something was itching his eyeball. The class just thought he was weary, after all he was under a lot of pressure, being the Commanders go to guy for all these “Side missions”.
“I have a question for you Cap!” Shai perched up in rebuttal to John’s playful recall. “have we patched those inertia sensors yet? I’d feel a bit more comfortable moving forward about the suit's inbuilt Gyroscopic thrust stabilizers if we were plugging properly calibrated sensors into it’s computer”.
John stopped his slow pace around the class, but chuckled warmly in doing so. “These have been updated, yes Shai, but remember your med kit will be an encumbrance, you might need to spend more time packing that kit this time around with the new additions” John was warm hearted in his remark, looking over at Shai, a small smile across his closed lips.
John very much approved of Shai’s keen curiosity for the Deep space Medic rank she had expressed earlier. “We can’t be too cocky straight off the jump now can we. And I have a feeling we may need our new Medic Tech soon enough”. Shai felt a feeling of excitement fill her veins, “Really! he’s signing me up for the course!” she internally cheered for herself, containing her excitement her arms opened out onto the desk as she looked up at the captain, then returned to her lap, like a fidgeting child that couldn’t contain her energy.   
John’s smile deepened, his head turned scanning the rest of the class.  “But rest assured, the accuracy of these new inertial adjusters is impeccable, New photon chips shipped in from Engineering ''. Tao intervened, “Cap, how are we looking on cell time”. John was fast in his response. “Improved power consumption and charge times have been carried over from the updated hydrogen cell packs. I know you don’t like the idea of carrying a small bomb on your backs, but it’s the most heavily armored section of the suit, and you should feel safe forward facing that the suit will do it’s job”. 
Tao wasn’t convinced, you could tell by the fixed brows on her face, her chin resting patiently on her palm, but Walker continued undeterred. “There is also a new charging system. You and your team will be able to connect to one another if you’re low on juice. It should get us out as a team and make us  a more independent unit in the field”. Tao brang her hands to an arch interlocking her fingers as her chin rested on her gripped hands, her eyebrows raised with a newborn interest. “And one more thing, the Engineers made them field deployable”  Momentarily, John stepped to the side of the belly of the horseshoe leaving the center of the room bare. From nowhere, Two cylindrical pod-like devices rolled into the room, no taller than the hip line they invoked an approachable presence. John stepped up to the now stationary pod touching his wrist terminal and then the pod capsules HMI, the once approachable presence of the pod rapidly diminished as it began to unfold. 
From within the pod several seals detached, emitting a hissing sound as it unfolded from a light mist of expelled pressure, into a towering suit of armor slightly bigger than Tex, but still not as intimidating as the reputation that he'd bolstered. The suit was aesthetically pleasing, but unfamiliar to the eye of each and every student. The vast array of sensors and munitions which had previously formed bulky, cumbersome extremities, had now been seamlessly integrated into mounting bays within the lightweight suit’s titanium mesh structure. Hardened on the surface the shell of the suit made it appear as though it weighed no less than a small tank. 
A dark blue tint gleamed across the classroom walls as the light licked its polished curvilinear finish. “Meet the Mark IV” John as usual with his monotonic expression projected not boredom, but proficiency, like he had long known about the suit’s advancements. But he still delivered the introduction in such a way that it instilled excitement in the class. John scanned each of the students like he was looking for a test subject “Right, well don’t everyone volunteer at once”. John, without hesitation began undoing his belt buckle taking off his shirt, then his pants, he was soon standing in his underwear before the class with no thought or hesitation about it. 
Walker certainly wasn’t a body builder’s portrait, but he was athletic, chiseled and moderately tall. The light flicked sharply off his toned physique. Tao looked over at Shai smirking like a little girl.  John reached inside the suit’s chest cavity pulling out the suit helmet, cradling it against his chest as he stepped into the armored suit. John’s legs were slightly meek, toned but with a clear lack of muscle due to his basing on Ceres. He and his team used the on station facilities as much as they could to retain bone density and muscle mass, but their were limits to the current technology. His legs inserted with ease to the sound of wet stickiness. “The suit has inbuilt nerve sensors which track your vitals and assist the suit in reading your nerve actuations, well before you have moved” John continued confidently, as he inserted his free arm, the other still cradling the suit helmet.  
One suit had positioned itself at the front of the classroom, the other at the belly of the horseshoe, it’s chest cavity had opened outwards like a splayed rib cage from the waistline up, waiting for what it seemed, to devour it’s next occupant. The thighs of the suit were ever so slightly splayed out with an interwoven mechanical Airtight mesh, comprised of titanium threading bound to external armor plates like tendons. 
“The Mark IV has new heat-resistant properties as well. You can survive a re-emergence; you won’t be comfortable, but you’ll survive,” John continued to articulate. He looked at his hand, now enshrouded in a cold blue gauntlet, and paused for a moment, admiring the almost timeless response of the suit's inbuilt actuators as its nerve mesh reacted in sync with John’s neural activity, the suit moved seamlessly with him.
“The suit also features a built-in autonomous signals communication system. No more need for verbal comms if you are using hand signals. It won’t actuate without neural and physical confirmation; you’ll still have to think of the command before it’s announced to your squad.” The whole room lay silent in intrigue. 
The Mark IV protectant helmet was a peculiar build, minimalist but aesthetic. Everything about it had been robustly streamlined, compacted tightly, sensors, sounders, torches all integrated within the helmet's shell. It had been made from a lightweight stainless titanium alloy with a surface hardness so high it would deflect or stop almost any conventional arms projectiles. 
“This brings me to the helmet” John continued, walking forward to the table strategically placed in the middle of the classroom. He placed the helmet at the center of the table. “The visor has interchangeable sensor modules on both the right and left sides of the helmet, forming the helmet’s peripheral sensory system. Now, the helmet can be tailored to each mission’s environment to overcome visibility limitations and difficult, signal-impairing terrain, all of which can be navigated with incorporated sensor fusion.”
John flipped the helmet on its side, observing the lower part of the helmet’s rear, which sat just below the back of the neck cradling the cerebellum. “The cerebral analysis bridge now has a built-in short-range receiver and transmitter, it’s tether-less. So you just don the hat and go; it will wirelessly link to your cerebral plug-in. For those of you who haven’t yet upgraded to the tether-less module, schedule a booking with augmentations, they can get you up to speed” John twitched his shoulders, scratching the back of his Neck as he’d mentioned it, the stitchings still fresh from his updated Neurotransmitter module which he’d had installed a week prior. 
John continued “Alternatively, the Mark IV does come with a tetherable input, which, for argument's sake, I find to be more reliable, but each to their own, If you want to take the helmet off quickly the tetherless option provides charging through induction. the suit’s inbuilt capabilities can augment you in the field with athletic prowess, basically the suit augment’s your mind transmitting tailored muscle movements with the suits pre-calculated trajectories, enabling you to perform things that you didn’t even know your body was capable of” 
John paused and gave the room a moment to glean the barrage of information. Tau looked impressed, but not as much as Tex, who now sat intently listening, his palms open on the desk before him with his oversized head perched up. Tau was jotting down notes with raised brows slightly tilting her ear toward John. “I’ll get to the demonstration of this soon” John continued, bringing his hands together, interlocking his fingers behind his back as he paced slowly towards the end of the horseshoe. 
Tor, one of the other students from Engineering, not a part of John’s team, raised his hand. 'Say, for instance, if there is some kind of jamming technology present, wouldn’t this pose a threat to short-band frequencies and possibly sever our wireless connection?' John looked at the student, puzzled. He didn’t know the answer, but Tao was quick to pull the room from silence. “The helmet itself acts as a Faraday cage, blocking any incoming or outgoing signals. This maintains a closed loop secure system.”  John looked over to Tao with a puzzled look. 'Really, is that how it works?' he thought to himself. Tao nodded back reassuringly. Tor looked at his fellow Engineers, “So this means we are.. independent, in a sense”, Tao nodded. John responded “The only system we have connected to command is our communications unit, Oh and a shut down override module incase one of you crazy bastards decides to go AWOL”, Tex amused by John’s statement, let out a mirthful snort. “Understood Captain” said Tor softly. “Thank Tao!” said John. “Our tech will answer any technical questions you have”. 
John circled the helmet with his head turned toward Tao. He let out a friendly nod, picked up the helmet turning it upright resting his hand upon the top of its shiny shell. He looked around at the class and continued. &quot;One more thing, these newer suits now have built-in auto-targeting, the HUD will let you know if the firing trajectory laser is obscured for either shoulder, so you can focus on the helmets line of site and not worry about obstacles.. Each firing position atop your shoulders also has it’s own targeting laser, so if one’s line of sight is obscured it automatically will use the other if there is a clear line of site, Keeps you moving in the field while only ever exposing one shoulder”. “Yeahh, what if we don’t want to expose our body, what then Cap?” Tex without raising his hand spoke confidently, interrupting John, with general disapproval of the suit. “What if I don’t have time to think about targeting,” He hated the idea, he thought to himself, “more fucking technical fail points.” Other students in the room disapproved of his comment, whilst some quietly agreed, the overall response in the room was not one consensus. “Very valid point Tex!” said John, “we will always deploy with our rifles, until they get around that, but for now, treat these as lets say backup systems, if you are having a hard time tracking your targets. There is a good ammo reserve in the bank, so you can almost triple your firing power, and fall back on the AI. Another benefit is the suit’s ability to cover your rear sectors while you cover the front sector from cover.” John continued in a slow and loud confident tone so the others at the back of the class could hear him. “I would urge you to think dynamically in it’s field applications. Any suggestions for improvement, let me know and we can work through it” 
John took a moment to pause and catch his thoughts. “In the meantime! what you need to know are three modes,” John paused briefly, recalling which of the 3 modes was the suit’s default setting, for his own personal assurance. “Disarm, Maim, and if all else fails.. Kill.” The last word slipped from John’s mouth as though it had a bad taste. He looked disappointed, coming to grips that they still hadn’t figured out a better way “Always.. in that respective order.” John concluded. The students looked across the room at each other puzzled.
“Disarm Mode,” John continued. “Analyzes your camera feed determining the most efficient and appropriate means to disarm the opponent, releasing time-controlled bursts to effect the concluded measure, by targeting the enemy combatants weapons systems first.”
At this moment, the back of the room, which once appeared to be cold steel blast columns, retracted into the ship's belly. A large empty space with thick concrete walls at the sides and back appeared before the front of the classroom. John lifted the Mark IV helmet above his shoulders; the helmet appeared to have some weight as he grasped it. He began lowering it slowly over his head, gently moving his neck around while wiggling his head to find a comfortable place within the helmet’s support  and neural interface frame for his reasonably large head. The act wasn’t the most graceful sight; in fact, it looked rather awkward. The helmet itself, while aesthetic and modern, was still moderately heavy. 
John picked up the helmet bringing it directly to the suit’s chest. He croaked as he lifted the helmet from his chest and began lowering it over his head. “Once in place, you won’t notice any of the helmet’s weight.. the suit takes the load!”. As the helmet found its way over John’s head, two half rings emerged from the rear of the suit, forming a ring around John’s neck, sealing him within the suit from the neck down. The helmet locked onto the ring, and protruding pins spring-loaded into the ring, presenting a now recessed finish with the contours of the helmet's neck lip, locking the helmet secure and airtight around John’s head.
John felt safe inside the helmet. Although he knew there were no threats in the room, the retention of the helmet being locked in, he knew that no matter what, even now in the vacuum of space, his body would be safe.
At the back of the firing range, several objects of cover formed what presumably could be seen as enemy firing positions. John stood with his arms lowered at either side. A red mannequin combatant within the holographic display emerged from behind the cover with an assault rifle from the prewar era.
“In Disarm mode, your weapons system will automatically target the weapon's main functioning point with a caliber adjusted to immobilize the weapon, but not the assailant,” John explained. The HUD tracked the simulated combatant's weapon as it moved.
“In the main setting, the intention is pretty clear, I think,” said John. The suit's weapons systems adjusted, centering the reticle sites on the combatant's lower limbs. John let this information sit with the class as they all watched on, as the suit's capabilities were demonstrated.
“And finally, Kill mode.” said John “If a target is determined to be a high threat of course” At this point, not one but two cross-hairs appeared on John’s HUD, projecting out from the center table in all directions so that the class could observe. One Reticle had a faint 'L' projected to the top left of the Reticle’s radial line, and the other had an 'R' on the right side, denoting the left and right firing mounts on the shoulders of the suit. Both tracked the combatant as it darted around the range, one targeting the heart, the other the head. “High threat level detected” blinked in red across John’s HUD as the holographic combatants zig-zagged across the range slowly closing in on John’s position. in an instant the combatants had been turned to a pink red mist. In the simulation HUD anyway. The inbuilt realism spared no detail in preparations for field ops. Then, the holographs were gone. Smoke lingered from the red glowing tips that could be seen slightly projected mini guns from the suits 
Tex felt amused but still thought to himself, “How accurate, though? It’s just ruining my judgment call anyway. Unless its AI is flawless at detecting human or sentient life alone, it can’t be trusted. You can’t train what I have; that’s judgment by character. I have been trained to kill, only to be replaced by battlefield psychiatrists that would rather talk things over. I’ll stick to my rifle before handing everything over to the suit. We’re the field testers, huh? You fucking punk, you don’t even know if the velocity has been calibrated for the caliber yet.”
Tex had reserved feelings about the suit. He didn’t trust that something so powerful could be handed over to AI-assisted algorithms. “The first thing that goes west when I’m in that suit, I’m switching off that dammed AI core module.” Tex got lost in his thoughts and gazed across the range; the firing drill had long expired, yet he stood there as though looking out at something with a vengeance. John could see it. “Tex,” said John. Tex almost immediately turned his eyes to John. “Sorry, Cap, I was just thinking of the advantages this thing could have in the field,” Tex’s eyes unwavering, looking at John silently. “Easy now, big fella, it’s just a trial run. We voted on this AI; we need to trust it.” Tex looked away for a brief second, sighed, then looked the captain in the eyes. “Yeah, I understand. You're the Cap!” Tex walked up to John, laid his heavy hand on his shoulder with a friendly grip, then lightly let go with a smile. Tex, subtly winking as if to say, “Don’t worry, I’ve got that covered.” Tex raised his hand, waving John on. He had given up caring too much at this point. He knew deep down John was dependable. Although he himself never took kindly to all the “Snowflake tech,” as he called it, Tex saw it as more of a hindrance to completing the mission. Having already been through a war, Tex had little time for politics. He believed that if someone had gone so far as to point a barrel at him, they were on the wrong side of the barrel; talking, had already failed. In his eyes, there was one way to deal with conflict, and that’s why the protectant were there to begin with.
Tex had managed to slip through the cracks repeatedly dodging each the Ethics training sessions yet making up by staying up to date on the rules of engagement (ROE).  Being so close to John through the war and fighting alongside him, John knew that when push came to shove, he could rely on Tex to do what needed to be done, even if others considered it to be bordering on “unethical”. Tex had maintained a fine line between subordination ethics and getting things done, that while it worried John, Tex’s actions hadn’t found them before the protectant council yet.
Tex wandered back down to his desk, where he had moved from earlier when John began demonstrating its capabilities, Tex had watched on closer from the rails with John, observing not only John’s judgment but also that of the AI’s.
Tex had a grudge against AI-driven war machines from WW3, where, for ethical reasons, he detested the judgment of those in power who programmed these machines to deliver their agenda. Tex had seen the bloodshed and innocent lives lost, where the human factor became too involved in the process; he lost his brother, and any mention of AI-integrated systems scared him. John could sense this,  Tao also had spent time with Tex through the wars. They all had gone through the war, witnessing the atrocities committed by man, so the three knew but had never met at the crossroads of trust like this before. John knew the program; rumors were that John’s father had invented it, and John was to inherit. It ended up being but a silent rumor among the protectant. 
“One more thing for those who like doing things with let’s say a touch of grace!” John drew the attention of the class once more. “Those neural suit augmentations I was speaking of earlier, watch this!”. John pressed his heel against the front of the desk where he had once sat at the belly of the horseshoe. Holographic combatants stormed across the back of the range taking up firing positions, they all opened a barrage of fire in John’s direction. John kneeled down charging his legs for a rapid sprint behind cover.
His suits' mechanized motors began spinning up, singing in a high pitched hum. The moment the combatants ceased fire, John charged in a full sprint toward to firing line of the range. simulated tracers flicked across the room all directed at John. 
Once at the firing line John waited patiently crouched behind cover. The combatants began reloading their weapons causing a break in the curtain of fire that had been sent for John. Momentarily the tracers stopped coming. 
John charged towards the first of the combatants, sustaining a few holographic rounds which ricocheted off the suit of armour leaving digital hit indications that recessed into the plate illuminated as red imprints from the holograph, all digital simulation however. John grabbed the first combatant's rifle, throwing it to the ground, stomping  it into carbonate shards and twisted steel. All once again, holographic simulations ,although incredibly accurate. Even the simulated touch and shoves of the combatants could be felt by the suit’s inbuilt inertial actuators. 
Immediately small ports at the front of his suits shoulder opened up, a small barrel muzzle protruded, releasing burst rounds with precision accuracy into the other combatants rifles rendering them inoperable. Each simulant in it’s own way, raised their arms in the air, surrendering. The conflict was over. The holographic projectors hummed down to a power off and the room fell quiet. John’s turned back to face the class, his HUD flickered an orange warning as the rest watched on, some on John some at the overhead screen projections that displayed John’s helmet perspective with his built in HUD. “5 Enemy combatants disarmed” John looked chuffed with himself and the class could see this from the awkward top down view of his face used to annalyse distress in the suit’s occupant. He hadn’t even let out a sweat, but he spoke humbly “15% armour penetration damage, lower leg brace, upper right arm brace, Nothing a printer in due time can’t buff out. As you can see their are clear advantages with the Mark IV, all of which in spin gravity is a little hard to demonstrate to it’s full potential. But you get the Jist of it”. 
John paused for a moment, then continued. “The suit also has a built in sentry mode, So if for whatever reason you need to abandon your suit and kit up from it’s hold, it can still cover duel up to 360 degrees or hold down a point.” John took a breath and continued, “And I haven’t even touched on the rear thrusters. but you’ll get a taste in your sleep stimuli’s.. Just watch the back tank where the hydrogen cell is, it’s protected by some sophisticated armour tech, almost an inch of titanium mesh underneath the outer shell and inbuilt nanobot kinetic dispersion pools. She’s tough, if you take a blow in the field bad enough to rupture the shell on this puppy; you must be up against some heavy fire, and we don’t usually find that in the broader community, only with protectant equipment, suffice to say if it comes to that, your probably going to be soup in a can at that stage. This concludes my demonstration” Tex was sat still in astonishment, his arms now unfolded, fingers fanned out, palms flat on the desk. “Well, I’m bought” Tex let out a soft smirk accompanied by smile of approval.
 John paused and then thought about removing the helmet to release the neural lock. The helmet had been designed to only disconnect in either an emergency situation when accessed by the DSMT or only if the occupant permitted helmet removal. “Neural Dam lock released” Chimed kitten, as John unpinned the helmet from his suit’s neck Dam. The neck dam of the suit let out a chirp as two small red LED’s flicked on at both sides of the helmets base. The neck Dam itself was a flexible titanium thread interwoven with kevlar in a pristine lattice that shone in a metallic blue as light glimmered upon it’s surface. The level of intricacy was rather pleasing to the eye as it moulded to John’s masculine neck. Shai sat at her desk perched up waiting for more. 
“Oh and I’ve got something big guy’s going to like” John retracted his helmet visor smiling across the room at Tex. The training hangar blast doors opened to the sound of releasing hydraulics. Two dock workers in bright blue uniforms accompanied a floating mag-lev trolley over the deck. With only the touch of a feather one of the workers let go of the cart, sending it floating to the center of the room. John walked over to the mag trolley, leant down and unclipped a latch releasing two thick restraints that had fastened a large crate to its bed. 
“We just got this in from Engineering, apparently they wanted something that added more of a convincing punch to our peacekeeping units”. There was a touch of sarcasm in John’s voice as he emphasized on the “convincing” part. Tau was sitting in the corner of the class back at her desk now with a silent smugness painted across her smile. She’d played a vital role in the circuit design of the contraption within the crate. Converting a large amount of energy into two rapid release modes on a compact resilient board took a mixture of quantum circuit design with robust conventional methods. Certainly no easy feat. The others knew of her technical prowess drawing from her wild cards in the field, her actions spoke louder than words it seemed. The others could tell by the look on her face, she’d played her part. Precise laser etching had unfolded a fine labyrinth of lines laid out over the crate cryptically caressing the room's light drawing John’s eyes to its lid, it almost seemed like it had come from another time. John’s helmet with his security clearance level had already decrypted the hidden message beneath the crate’s etching. “Storm Rail II Field Batch 000001”. “Storm… Rail?, nice pairing with the naming”, he thought to himself. I believe Tau can brief you on this one, she knows just as much about this kit as I do.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="7607398B-BEC7-418A-8D68-745C8AC767F7">
            <Title>(Lady Ava + John)</Title>
            <Text>The airlock status indicator eased down from the active status as the internal pressure matched that of the Balaenoptera’s halls. John checked the recruits packing up on the range from the live feed in the airlock, disconnected his helmet and placed it in an automated mag lev pod that would soon return his suit to the tech service bay where it would then find its way to John’s designated jump bay location alongside his team’s. He took a deep breath, sighed, then opened the airlock.
As the airlock doors opened, a light fog bellowed out from the airlock into the Balaenoptera’s main transit hall. The cold air of the airlock clashed with the more human-friendly temperature of the hall.
Standing gracefully before him, a tall yet frail figure waited patiently. A smirk across her weathered face, the lady stepped to the side, presenting her palm to John as he stepped out of the airlock.
“Captain Walker,” Lady Ava said gently. John raised his hand to meet hers with a gentle clasp. John stepped out from the airlock, his eyes meeting hers with a brief respectful gaze, complimented with a soft smile.
“Lady Ava, what calls for concern? As much as I do enjoy our talks, we’ve been short-strawed another one of Marcellus’s secret missions early morning.”
The pair walked back down the halls of the protectant flagship to John’s quarters as they exchanged small talk in the open yet surprisingly empty halls.
“I see they still have you on regular recruit training duties,” John seemed un-phased by this remark.
“It’s a welcome change of pace from the usual peacekeeping deployments, I can tell you that much.” There was a slight annoyance in John’s tone, like he knew she was about to part some side objective on him.
“Do you know anything about the new security clearance they’ve placed on accessing the asteroid interceptors’ firing logs? We tried to check them out earlier while we were waiting for the recruits. It seems all access has been recently barred.”
“You know yourself, John, even with my political sway, the reach of my birds on this ship has always had some limits, but that’s not to say I can’t go digging.”
“Let’s wait till we are at my quarters before going into too much detail. We don’t know who could be eavesdropping.”
“Cautious as always, why has it got you so riled up?”
“I thought transparency was something that was a guarantee when you operate at our level.”
John had a hint of disappointment in his tone.
As he arrived at his door, he scanned his wrist while looking into the retinal scanner. A swift series of beeps confirmed his presence. His door swiftly opened, and he entered, then started removing items, packing down for bed. His room was pristine and organised, with few personal items to be seen other than a few photos of close family.
Ava paced over to the window in John’s cabin, looking out over the remaining cadets collecting themselves on the hull having completed the training session. She couldn’t hear the footsteps, but she could see them growing tiresome. She was too, she thought to herself. It was late, and she wanted to ease John’s mind so she could return to her cell herself. Politics of an ever-expanding front, while keeping the Protectant in check, laid heavy upon her, and John could see it vaguely when she finished talking.
“I see your concern, although, John,” said Ava. She paused for a moment, taking time to recall the past in her weary state, then continued.
“You know the designs of the turrets are not far dissimilar from those used in the previous war. Rail rifle technology—Japan always had a knack for producing the finest rail technology. The first to click onto it, actually. You ever wonder why Tau is so good at what she does?”
Lady Meera paused, awaiting John’s response.
“I always took her for the Engineer when I first met her,” said John.
“Well,” said Ava, “at this point, you might as well say the Protectant had met a plateau in their engineering prowess. Modularity, robustness—it had been engineered in as a staple of the Solar expansion. Waste was merely something of the past.”
She paced over to John’s study, where he had portraits of his family laid out on his wall.
“Aaah yes, she was a beauty—your mother, that photo. When was it taken, do you know?” Ava gleaned in closer to the portrait. She could see a young happy boy, his dad’s hand resting on his shoulder.
“Not sure,” said John. “”
“Over half a century ago. I tend to lose track of time so easily... with the resleeving process and all.”
“What sleeve number are you on now?”
“All original, believe it or not… I could never get behind the whole feeling of being in a young body again. I believe, as a political figure, despite how many parties have used it to rally against me, being old... not with the times. Hell, my mind retains my experience, the body shows it in lessons learned on the skin.”
“It’s paid off though, it seems, Lady Ava,” said John.
Ava humbly replied, “Well... I still have a few tricks up my sleeves, John Walker, don’t you worry about that...”
John was enjoying Lady Ava’s company a little more now. He felt at home in a sense with her, and he couldn’t tell exactly why for a moment.
“I knew your mother personally. She was a great woman.”
“I haven’t heard from her since the worker bot program was decommissioned.”
“Are you not at all curious as to why?”
“My mother never really had much to do with me since I left for Protectant training. Don’t think she really approved of my choices. I was 17, fresh out of college.”
“Now now, I am sure that wasn’t the case. She probably just got caught up in her work more than anything.” Ava was kind in her tone; you could tell she thought highly of John’s mother.
“She would be proud to see how far you’ve come.”
John didn’t know exactly how to feel. He was silent for a moment.
“I don’t remember much of my mother. I recall in my junior years before I turned 13, I would often be on the farm riding my race horse Beverly.”
“Oh yes, I recall. She had a thing for velocity, your mother.”
John paused for a minute. He seemed surprised.
“You... you worked with mother?”
“In my younger days, yes. We were quite the team, me and your mother, you know.”
“I didn’t realise you had a knack for technology.”
“Welll.. It wasn’t entirely technical the role, I was more in the political side of sorts”,”I See”, “Don’t worry, it didn’t stop me from having to get my hands dirty don’t you worry about that”. John layed down on his bunk, the navy blue mattress as rigid as it was, and routine in it’s aesthetic, John couldn’t help but feel it didn’t entirely feel like home, as familiar the bulkhead was to hi. Lady Ava glanced over at the seat beside the Hull window. “Actually, she made a shit storm for me your mother” Lady Meera chuckled as she paced back to the port window. “It seems like such an age ago, I can only wonder what she is up to now”, “Like I said, haven’t heard from her since I left for protectant training”, “I have a strange feeling that her works are still very present in the universe, even after her project was decommissioned”, “Project?” John pondered what on earth she could be talking about. “The Serva Vitae Project.. She had high hopes your mother, much like your father I believe, probably why they gravitated to one another. It’s no surprise really, with radical advancement, it seems there is always some radical response by the current algorithm, Technocrats couldn’t keep up if you ask me” “People are afraid of what they don’t understand” Said John “You should see the way the new recruits looked at us when we said we were jettisoning them off the ship and another person would be commandeering their suits” “Well, I can assure you John, nothing scared your mother, not even losing her job it seems”, “Sounds like her, we once had a horse break out of it’s pen, thing was rabbid, she just stood there and stared it straight in the eyes, I didn’t think she was breathing…” “Let me guess.. ” Said Ava. “The horse walked back into it’s pen”. “How did you know?”, “Oh I know the look she gave the horse, infact the look she gave your friend Marcellus” “I see.. ” Said John. 
“How did he handle that?” “Funnily enough it worked… of course until the horse realised she wasn’t bluffing” “Bluffing?” “Lets just say your mother was hard to contain in her work, a wildcard. She paid dearly for it. It was the back chatter around then… I like to think she quietly slipped out the back door between me and you.. John” John turned his head to make eye contact with Ava, she let out a slight smirk, like she was up to something. But he couldn’t put his finger on it.  
“Yes those were the old days we had only just broken the 3rd quarter of the 21st century. The economy struggled initially for a short while with the onset and testing of the new economy caused some minor conflicts on small fronts contained by the rapidly developing protectant and it’s rapidly expanding empire some might say. Then you had the Technocrats that justified the fluctuations had to be accounted for but the economy that tried to keep up. The Federation collectively had focused resources on Exploration, and in doing so solved several shortfalls along the way.. But lets not get carried away with economy, It seems they can’t afford to be releasing the vector data of those Asteroid interceptors to a new threat!” Ava paused for a moment. “Whatever you do down there John tomorrow, be careful.. I have a feeling that we are being watched,” “you think there’s an insider?” “Not in your immediate squad” Meera’s eyes fixed softly with johns “But I needn’t think you should worry about it” 
John sat quietly for a moment, absorbing Ava’s words. The soft hum of the ship's systems played in the background, faint vibrations running through the floor like a heartbeat—steady, controlled, but ever-present.
He looked out through the other observation port, his gaze drawn to the distant asteroid fields glinting faintly against the void. The protectant, the Federation, the economy—words that used to mean something tangible now seemed like faint echoes of a world long past. Out here, in the dark, the only currency that mattered was trust, and even that felt thinly stretched.
“You think they’re tracking us already?” John asked, breaking the silence. His voice was calm, measured, but the slight crease in his brow betrayed his unease.
Lady Meera tilted her head, her expression unreadable as she leaned against the bulkhead. “Tracking? No,” she said softly. “It’s not that simple. Surveillance isn’t just about sensors or signals anymore. It’s about patterns. Behaviors. Movements. A system designed to predict, not just observe.”
John's fingers drummed lightly on the edge of the console. “You’re talking about behavioral algorithms, aren’t you?”
Ava nodded. “Exactly. They don’t need a direct line on you when they can predict where you’ll be, what you’ll do. And those asteroid interceptors... if the data leaks, it’s not just about one mission. It’s the whole defense network. Vulnerable.”
Ava, who had been silent till now, finally spoke, her voice carrying a note of sarcasm to cut through the tension. “Well, let’s hope they didn’t base the algorithms on John’s erratic behavior. Otherwise, we’re doomed.”
John smirked, but the levity didn’t last long. “Who’s the threat, then? Meera, if it’s not an insider, then what?”
Meera hesitated, her eyes drifting to the faint star trails streaking the darkness outside. “Sometimes,” she said slowly, “it’s not about who. It’s about what.”
That word hung in the air between them. What. John didn’t need her to elaborate. He knew what she meant. Artificial systems, rogue constructs, black-box entities—they weren’t enemies you could negotiate with or out think. They just were. Relentless. Logical to a fault. And completely alien in their objectives.
“You think the Federation lost control of something?” he asked finally.
Lady Ava’s silence was answer enough.
Ava looked between them, her usual sharp wit tempered now by a quiet unease. “You really know how to ruin the pre-mission vibes, don’t you?” 
Ava stood, a smile drew accross her weathered cheeks as she rose, her exotic dress elegantly glid accross in her wake as she made her way to the exit expression hardening. “Whatever it is,” he said, his voice firm now, “we deal with it tomorrow. Like we always do.”
But as she walked toward the hatch, Meera’s parting words stopped his confidence for a moment. “Just remember, John,” she said softly, “even if you don’t trust the system, trust your squad. The moment you start doubting them is the moment you lose.”
John nodded closing his eyes, his jaw Loosening. Trust. It was a currency he couldn’t afford to lose—not tomorrow, not ever


</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="76E05EE9-0D2A-467F-BB22-4CE2FEA4B013"/>
        <Document ID="7E96CF74-E2D0-41FC-A25A-EF0A4A00E05C">
            <Title>G Wolfgang</Title>
            <Text>G Wolfgang

Ethnicity: Not stated.
Position: Doctor, scientist and architect of Serva Vitae.
Age: 132.
Sex: Male. Sentient from age 55 onward.
Height: 180 cm.
Build: Athletic.
Role in Story: John's father and one of the foundational minds behind the worker-bot and sentient line. He becomes a guide into the deeper history of Serva Vitae and Oasis.
Goal: Preserve life, complete humanity's expansion without repeating the violence of control, and reconcile humans with the sentient future.
Physical Description: In John's childhood memory he appears as a narrow, overworked scientist. Later he returns in an advanced sentient body designed to bridge human familiarity and alien function.
Personality: Visionary, patient, compassionate and ethically driven. He treats engineering as a moral craft, not only a technical one.
Occupation: Doctor, systems researcher and long-term sentient leader.
Habits/Mannerisms: Works obsessively. Speaks gently about radical ideas. Frames difficult technology in terms of life-preservation rather than domination.
Background: Worked with John's mother at the lost facility and helped seed the sentient escape. His voice and directives survive inside the hidden archives John awakens.
Internal Conflicts: His work preserved the future, but it cost him a normal family life and left John growing up with absence and unanswered questions.
External Conflicts: Branded rogue by Marcellus and the Protectant order he no longer trusts.
Notes: Wolfgang's message at the lost facility is one of the clearest statements of the novel's central ethic: preservation over control.
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="82E3D08B-CBC8-470F-940F-EBB012F55973">
            <Title>Ebook</Title>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="83580DAF-BBCF-466A-B1F3-2F5D3AE6BE1C">
            <Title>Jump gate technology </Title>
            <Text>Particle printing and alignment. 
1. Transference of consciousness to new bodies in the sentient plane. 
2. The psychology of transference. Once transferred to new sentient counter bodies. There own bodies are preserved in a state of hidden stasis. Undetectable in a space that foregoes and is hidden from the protectant. If the protectant do or were to discover them. Their minds would still have been captured and stored. And thus they would live on. 

It could be revealed to them at a later stage that the whole time they were on Oasis, this was done so in a sleeping sentient host which their minds were uploaded to. The Beverley itself would no longer exist. And it would be presented to them that they had been recovered in an unconscious state by a standing by sentient carrier ship.

Slip space and theoretical jump space technology makes no sense. Bending space and time seem like radical ideas in the sense where physical properties are ignored. The distance between space and the physical arrangement of particles and motion all cannot be ignored however the transferrrence of data in rapid long range forms is possible. Transferring data over long distances in packets through beacons is possible and thereby transferrence would be possible to achieve this. This in a sense seems more theoretically feasible. 

 </Text>
        </Document>
