        <Document ID="BC07ED16-787D-464B-B55C-F0E29E6C651C">
            <Title>John's Drop Ship (The Icarus)</Title>
            <Text>The Icarus

Capacity: Small corvette complement built around John's squad; exact numbers not stated.
Role in Story: John's dependable drop ship and the team's main human vessel before the sentient transition.
Related Characters: John Walker, Tao Vang, Shai Siante, Tex Kovachnikov.
Season: Chapters 2-4.
Unique Features: Old but battle-proven systems, extremely tight internal layout, and deep crew trust built on familiarity rather than novelty.
Description: The Icarus is John's flag ship and the tightest corvette in the fleet. It lacks flashy specialist amenities, but John trusts it precisely because its older systems are dependable and familiar. It carries the squad through high-altitude insertions and remains the team's dependable human corvette during the lost-facility mission.
Sights: Cold steel trusses, amber jump-light over the launch bay, cramped troop hold, and a hangar tight enough for Tex to complain about.
Sounds: Engine hum, ramp mechanisms, launch-bay intercoms and gear clamps snapping into place.
Smell: Cold metal, regulated air and machine oil.
Notes: The manuscript's explicit self-healing hull sequence belongs to the Beverly, not the Icarus.
Built at: Protectant fleet service.
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="BF253840-4071-45D8-BD2A-35B72D27DFA3">
            <Title>LRM incoming</Title>
            <Text>
“I’ve detected a long range missile heading straight for us”. Tao paused almost as if in shock at what she was reading on her HUD. “John.. the signature is giving me a 68% likelihood It’s protectant” She looked up at John in disbelief “Marcellus?” John pulled open his HUD and began reading over what Tao was picking up on her HUD. Beads of sweat ran down Tex’s forehead. Shai Tao and Tex looked back at each other then looked back to John. Almost as if to ask if anyone had a solution  “Does anyone know what the fuck is going on” said Tex. “We’ve got 4 minutes until this place is buried in the mountain” Tao was busy plotting an exit route with the help of Kitten in her wrist terminal. The once silent halls leading up to the conversion chamber they all stood steadfast in now spoke as countless blast doors along the hall rapidly closed shut entombing them deeper within the mountain. John looked back at his Mark IV and began climbing back into its gelatinous mould. Small sparks, not vicious but like a silent glow illuminated around his carapace as he slid into its hold. He was calm and quiet in doing so, like nothing was happening. “I’m locked out of the Dam mainframe!” Tao’s voice echoed back to them in the vast laboratory with intensity, beads of sweat ran down her forehead. Shai quivered in her suit, she felt her hairs tense as goosebumps ran down her spine, then she tracked John walking across the room to the blast door in his Mark IV. 
John placed his hand on the blast door, but this time instead of forcing the seams apart, he just rested it there as though to be listening. He ejected his suit’s interface chord and plugged into the terminal at the wall next to it, the tentacle-like coils reaching out like sniffers found their connection’s almost intuitively. “Give me a second”. John felt himself flowing through the facility once more. He closed his eyes and found himself transgressing the walls, flying through a grid marking nodes on each blast door as he raced through the illuminated route Tao had planned. He could see himself and his allies in the room, the others eagerly looking on at him as he stood against the blast door like a statue in a sleep-like state. Then, it was done. The blast door jolted open and John stepped out easing into a run. The others all looked at each other, Then they fell in formation behind him. Tex at the back shrugged his shoulders and followed. 
“Seems like you’ve done this before cap” Tex felt uneasy by John’s sudden newfound familiarity with the facility. “Yeah I don’t know” John didn’t seem bothered by the steady run along the route Tao had planned out, speaking without fault or pause as each upcoming blast door seemed to open in anticipation of his approach. “I feel like I’ve been a part of this facility since it’s fruition, like it’s a part of my body. I seem to be able to speak to her”, “Ooh k chief, understand. Well, at least we know you’re on our side and maybe she’s on our side too”, “It would seem that way, whoever left this place. They either wanted it to land in the right hands or they had a plan in case it landed in the wrong hands” John paused for a moment. “I’m hoping that the latter isn’t the case, and that fucking missile is completely independent of the facilities lock down procedure”.
“On that missile, we’ve got three minutes to get out of here before it becomes our tomb” Tao raised her rifle up siting the deep halls ahead, slowing her pace behind John “John.. the floating Pods are just up ahead”. John seemed unphased by Tao’s statement. “I know” said John, keeping his pace. Tao didn’t know what to expect on the other side or what the Pod’s were there for in the first place. On their rapid descent past the pods they had a short glimpse of the floating structure hanging between the mountains. “One more blast..” John had stopped up ahead, Tao had all but forgotten what she was going to say, slowing her pace arriving just behind John. The others arrived behind her momentarily heaving and coughing catching their breath. John seemed unphased, breathing normally. The sound of grinding steel came to a halt as the final blast door retracted into the mountain, a dim evening light with daggers of cold wind ice and snow pelted them simultaneously, stinging but inviting them out. Their suit’s compensated for the push of the blizzard winds, digging their heels into snow piled atop steel grates that stretched outwards past the mountainous cavern's mouth. Icy winds grasped John as he walked slowly toward the cavern mouth, the others observed the a blanket of ice forming over his suits shoulders as he pushed slowly forward
“Missile Incoming! ETA 1 minute, 30 seconds” strobed on their HUDS. For a moment the Blizzard went silent, opening up the evening night sky. It was all too mesmerising as they looked on, they lost all touch with the gravity of their surroundings. A plethora of colours stars and nebulas stared back at them, so clear. The team took a moment to appreciate the beauty of how far the human race had come. Towering wires suspended a snake-like structure bypassing the saddle of the mountain valley below. The “Pods” Tau had referred to earlier, hung between suspended walkways like nodes from wires reaching up above that shot to the higher ridges of each mountain face, suspending the structure. It was impressive to anyone who had never seen it clearly before. Encapsulated amidst the constant fog of the mountains, the suspended structure extended and weaved following the valley for some time until it turned again, connecting to the face of the cliff on the other side. A silhouette of dark velvet purple flicked with specks of white like waves opening up to blues then bright pinks, they felt their souls riding the stars in another universe, then they were pulled back. “John, this valley is never this clear! they’re beaming our location so the missile can maintain it’s fix!” John observed a slight radiation spike on his HUD then looked ahead at the first Pod and began charging toward it, the others falling in rapidly behind him. “I’ve seen what’s on the other side, it takes us deep into the northernmost mountain. We should be able to seek shelter on the other side within the mountain then make our way down to the saddle” John now panted as the words spilled out from him with haste. There wasn’t any more time to explain. He made it to the first Pod and heard a jilt as the motors seized. The ice on the first Pod’s blast door was so thick that it had locked the blast door closed. 
&quot;Fuck, it’s completely frozen over,” John punched the blast door, clenching his teeth in frustration as his jaw grinded to a lock. Nothing happened, but a the slightest crack nuanced the surface of the murky dirt strewn ice that had entombed the pod .
He stepped to the side of the floating grate, gripping one of the fixed wire ropes that the structure and his team hung so delicately from in the treacherous wind. John veered over the roof of the pod with determined eyes, gripping the steel rope firmly in his gauntlet. He placed his other hand just above the blast door, then began pulling himself up in an attempt to reach the pod's roof.
The weight distribution of the suit felt awkward. Shards of ice cracked from the rope, his gauntlet sliding down to meet the edging of the grate as his body followed. John felt as though he had begun to fall indefinitely, immersed in another time, falling backwards looking up at the night sky. The valley floor awaited his fragile shell, and he could see the wailing arms of the worker bots that had just been tossed off the bridge as they fell momentarily after him, meeting the same fate in what felt like the end of a bad dream.
As he looked up, he could make out a familiar figure glaring back at him. Blue eyes, cold like steel, the dead glow of night sharpening their glare, gripped his soul. “Captain!” The menacing blue eyes vanished into thin air.
Shai had placed her hand underneath his armpit and began lifting him to his feet from where he had fallen hard on the grate. John came to, finding his gaze fixed on the bottom of the valley below. He could see a misty fog with a subtle blue glow, which he assumed to be some strange reflection of the evening sky, yet peculiar.
“We need to move!” Shouted Shai. John shook his head, disoriented and shocked at the vision he had just seen, then looked to his HUD. “Guess it’s time to put the suit to the test.”
Shai stepped back after lifting John to his feet. She knelt down, lined up the landing DZ on the roof of the Pod from her fly-by scan earlier before they completed their dives. Shai carefully focused with her mind on the directions to move the landing marker on the scan with her neural interface. She managed to select in her mind 3 variables by imagining the numbers. She focused. “10, 4000, 2000”. As she thought of the arrow xyz coordinates, she pinpointed the center of the 1st pod's roof. She imagined herself gliding with the assisted thrusters, easing her down onto the next roof.
“See you 3 over there,” the words escaped her with a shaky confidence as she stirred with thoughts of all the interacting systems that could fail her, as they had with previous suits. She charged back her legs, lowering her center of gravity closer to the ground, ready to pounce.
The sound of high pressure fluid hummed as her hydraulic drive pipes powered up from her suit’s hydrogen combustion engine. High pitched hissing and pops ever so silently eased down as her drive pipes charged. Hydrogen engines were vastly adapted and the newer suits dampening made them again even quieter than their gasoline counterparts. 
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="BF9234D6-B9BC-4986-BAE8-79A50AF7B8CD">
            <Title>(Shai) Mark IV life support mesh</Title>
            <Text>“Eeerr Thanks Tao, much appreciated”, Shai shot up out of her seat awkwardly making her way to the front of the horseshoe, lightly stepping to the middle of the class where John had rested the suit’s helmet. The suit stood strong next to the desk now, an open shell waiting for it’s next occupant. As John made his way back to end of the horseshoe, He lifted his hand and patted Shai on the shoulder as she passed him by, she let out a little thank-you smile, followed by a deep breath, she hated public talking, but she also took pride in her craft, she got a great sense of satisfaction form sharing her knowledge with the others. 
“Ok! so Deep space medicine varies a little differently to your standard medicine, and as I am sure you are all aware, having lived on Ceres you have done this training more times than you can remember'', Shai spoke with deliverance in her message, then paused momentarily “But.. Complacency is the killer..” She concluded softly resting her hands in each other. She no longer seemed awkward the deeper she got into her field.
She knelt down in front of the suit, her small frame seemingly smaller before the scale of the armoured giant.  Shai detached a matt white vambrace from the suit’s forearm .The brace had a blue cross on it’s side, what had become the universal symbol for a Deep Space Kit (DSK) med kit, Specifically designed for the Deep Space Medic or ‘DSM’ as she explained it. The DSK didn’t contain standard bandages or Gauss. It had been designed for zero gravity situations. 
The braces thickness and solid gloss finish made it feel and looked like an angelic piece of armour crafted by the heavens, although it was something else in its design, much more if used properly by it’s occupant. Shai held the bracer out in front of her “Has anyone used a DSK Medi-Guard?” she caressed the shell of the vambrace, running her finger down its white glossy finish. “The Medi-Guard comes stock standard with the Mark IV suit. Most of its operation is intuitive and based off your suits vital monitoring systems linked through your cerebral gate”
Shai admired the beauty of the system. She seemed possessed, as she slowly rose from her knee. She took the brace in both her palms and continued. “For the most part the unit is autonomous, although the under belly does have a hold for manual administration, and it’s drug stores are removable like so” Shai pressed her finger on a small latch removing a seamless cover from the brace. Underneath a series of pipes fed into a stack of vials that surrounded the braces inner shroud. 
She plucked one of the vials from it’s holder and inserted it into a syringe in a fluid motion, “So that they can be administered by trained persons” She pressed the syringe needle into a square replica of human intramuscular tissue, injecting the contents of the vial.
“What happens if the module gets taken out by incoming fire” Tex, folded his arms, a slight smirk rocking back in his chair, his head slightly tilted forward.. “Good question Tex” Shai said softly. “If the module takes damage it’s fine” She continued almost unmoved by the big guy’s question, re coupling the DSK back to the suits forearm folding it over the vambrace and locking it back in place. “The vambrace merely acts as a backup module for easy access and patient treatments. There is a physical separate overlay in your HUD, If the DSK has been damaged, Don’t worry too much, all of the core programming for the neuro-swap run’s from your neural implant in synchronous function with your suit. The helmet’s HUD can also be used to select pre-programmed procedures.
Nano-Bot holding bays are built into the suits rear around the lower abdomen, Heart analysis and sounding is performed  through an augmented chest cavity with listeners, coupled to your cerebral link from your implant, appropriate medicinal inputs are distributed through a venous injection mesh spanning the entirety of the suit, all of which is controlled by one central point, the helmets computer. The bracer, simply acts as a backup for field deployment if all else fails. All the knick knacks to keep you going in the thick of it, are stored in the lower rear of the suits backed armour plating, so it is completely safe. Measured dosages are automatically placed into injection ports in the suit. These Injection ports feed through an intricately woven path through the suit that follows much the same pattern as the nervous system. Think of your veins for instance, they have hundreds of gates. This system is assessed by your suit’s inbuilt nerve scanners. If you are injured the suit performs a measured intramuscular injection of adrenaline, or be it paralysis to an affected limb, the injection needle acts as a channel for nerve tendrons which take over and act in place of severed nerves to bridge gaps in motor function through nerve damage. The suit gives you a temporary backup nervous system, and its actuators can still function even if you have taken neural damage to your muscular, or spinal system”. 
Shai went quiet for a moment, pondering “I wonder what the limits and protections are built into this thing if a cognitive hack takes place, but who would have the technology to do such a thing, commandeer our own autonomous system through our suits? I need to check this with Tao later” Shai wasn’t entirely convinced of the helmets security protection, it didn’t cover all grounds, or sleeper programs that could be laid when the suits are sent out of production. 
She looked concerned for a minute, then resumed back to her lesson. “So, to summarize. In a traumatic situation, the suit’s integrated neural mesh with equipped motor response actuators offers crucial medical response benefits. The neural integration enables a more natural and intuitive control over the suits movements. This enhances coordination and suit responsiveness, eliminating any delays of the earlier kinematic models. This is especially valuable for individuals that may sustain impaired motor functions during a conflict,  allowing them to regain mobility and independence in the fight. 
Additionally, the neural interface facilitates better synchronization between your intentions and the suit, reducing cognitive effort to operate the suit. This is also vital in high-stress situations. Overall the effectiveness of this suit is ahead of its time in medical and emergency scenarios and you my friends, will basically have a walking life suit until we can get you to a med bay to repair any severed tissue. This suit will get you home” Shai rose from her pose of admiration where she had knelt in front of the suit’s shell, she had been holding in her elbows the new helmet cradling it like a child, gazing into the chip integration pad that was recessed into the titanium mesh at the back of the helmet. She appeared like a mother before the class, and they felt embraced to have such a knowledgeable medic with them. 
John sat patiently observing Shai, she was so nimble, yet her mind was beautiful to him, he smiled knowing how safe his team would be with her watching over them. “Shai, so tell us about the buddy system feature, the one that’s paired with each suit”, “Yes Cap!” Shai woke from her deep thoughts and returned to the room. “When an injured ally has taken damage these suits have a neural link system, So say if we need to utilize a certain skill set we don’t have as long as someone with the skills are in range they can piggy back onto the suits backup neurological pathway and in essence hijack the suit, to well get you out of a pickle. Think of it like swapping suits really”. 
Tau lent in with edging curiosity, “So you are saying we can swap bodies?”.Tex let out a cheeky smirk, “I’ve always wondered what it would be like to have control over a woman's body, can we do a test run?”, Tau rolled her eyes with slight amusement. “Focus guys!” Shot John. 
Shai continued pretending to be uninterrupted. “The big guy actually raises a good point. The suit doesn’t allow you to swap bodies as such Tex.. It just projects your motor commands onto the other suit and if you permit it your suit will take their commands over yours, You can choose a two way bridge or a one way which may be the case if your friend is unconscious... It isn’t a body swap as such, but a suit swap. Because these suits no longer rely on Kinematic but cerebral actuation, The cerebral gate intercepts signals from the host projecting it to the required suit via it’s home suit’s telemetry unit, as long as it is within range. Once you have used your own suit long enough, your synapse profile will be projected with your cerebral communications hand in hand. So even though your host may have a better knee than you or a kink in his elbows connective tissue, you will experience your neural commands all the same regardless of the suit you commandeer” 
Tau cut in. “It is a little jumpy at first if you have never synced with a suit before though as each suit needs to adjust for different neural signal latencies of different hosts.. So tonight your rides may be a little bumpy while it smooths out your synapse profile, So don’t get to cocky on the outer hull with the first run”, “Thanks Tau” said Shai. 
“Obviously if a swap is performed with an unconscious ally your suit will be incapacitated taking on their state. So I would advise thinking things through before establishing the bridge. On that thought.. Guys would it be worth using the Mark IV’s new auto targeting system in this case?”, “Nice pickup Shai!” Tao was impressed with Shai’s lateral thinking. “I’ll jot down a note and get to work on putting that feature into the suits programming tonight, then forward it onto engineering for testing”. “I took the liberty of setting a reminder for you Miss Vang” Chimed Kitten. 
“Ok I think it’s time we gave the legs a stretch” John piped in with a soft smile. “Sure Cap, I’ll let you run through the pre-walk checks” Shai bowled slightly at the captain, stepping aside to the air lock panel and began prepping the interlock over the panel.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="C004A8DC-C6DD-4123-B9F4-1662F145FA61">
            <Title>On the bridge of the Docs Sentient flag ship</Title>
            <Text>
John stood by Doc looking out over the strange planets horizon. The stratosphere seemed to move, deceiving new eyes into believing a concoction of gases may be stirring. Although beneath the ozone 150 klm down what moved was very real. “Oasis lives in a constant state of flux, each of it's surface plates shifting like a moving ocean, exchanging in a constant symbiosis with other plates much like cells passing by in a venous system. Each with its own eco system interacting with one another giving and taking in a never ending wheel of life.” John found it hard to fathom what he was hearing trying to piece together the biology of it all, he listened quietly in marvel as the planets surface danced a slow display from outer orbit, what ensued on the surface moved much faster. Doc could see through Johns gaze for what it was, curiosity, he obliged it. 
“Initially we lost many resources on our first settlement, to be expected. New species of local fauna didn't take to fondly passing through to new structures prying and investigating finding new ways to penetrate our boundaries. You would be surprised how aware some creatures are”. 
In the centre of the bridge a holograph rotated above a circular well which recessed below the Ignotus’s bridge deck fitted with countless light emitting modules. The holograph emitted a light blue grow across the room drawing any occupants to its centre as it caught their eye. John found himself turning to find the massive bulbous head of a “Spychottidal” sweeping the bridge radius pass by him. “Heightened awareness, Spychottidals are genus to mineral rich plate types 346 and 348. They have been known to venture out to other plates in search of new mineral rich environments. Their heightened sensory system can be attributed to a mineral rich diet that is used in the amplification of their sensory nodules found within their hardened bulbous neural core atop an elongated body used to burrow deep down to mineral sources” John read through the creature's description gleaning deeper into the struggles faced with the Sentient colonisation.
“Spychottidal's were able to see past our improved mesh technology, we tried neural blockers and eventually found seismic barriers could only be used in several cases. Although the shifting of the plates gave us some time to recover and learn from Interactions we had to plan our resources and work around the shifts. Our expansion at times has had its setbacks environmentally where we have had to improvise, however if we keep the balance with environmental markers we read to check our presence has minimal impact on the planet itself, this means that the symbiotic tracer we leave behind and serve to gain from the existing system flourishes” John seemed impressed by what the foresight the algorithm was capable of projecting, How something made from sheer resources pulled from within a planet itself, inorganic could bolster such a profound respect for it’s or his organic counterpart. Although he knew the amalgamation before him to be his father, He couldn’t help but feel like he was something more, an entity he had created and merged with. “”

</Text>
        </Document>
