        <Document ID="F5B7A3B1-6A1A-4403-86B5-A463C1FAD0ED">
            <Title>Deep Space Training</Title>
            <Text>The Earth federation had set up an efficient system that supported human life, however the scarcity of resources and the reach to attain further and newer minerals, had extended the outer reach of the federation's presence in the Solar system, or what they often referred to as SOL-1. It did not make sense to attribute resources to terraforming, instead deep space travel was deeply invested in, targeting skip space which piggy backed off stationed gravity recharge points outside of each main planet within SOL-1. The current concluded best path forward from AI driven time prediction’s and Engine technology scaling and revolutionary research were detecting new channels of energy within SOL-1. The Federation's mission was now to colonize a planet with breathable air, in a neighboring solar system. 
It was more resourceful , to inhabit the possibility of making it to a planet which no longer required Terra-forming. John entered the classroom, where he was met by 19 other students. John was 20. And on this occasion last to arrive having been called to the bridge for a brief with Commander Marcellus. “Sorry for my late attendance” Said John, “Business with Commander Athos”. John then proceeded to his seat at the end of the table, his name chauffeured across a tag. The tables in the room formed a horse-shoe, allowing for the instructor to easily and leisurely stroll in front of his students with technical equipment. John found himself at the back of the classroom. “I like this.. Quiet” John thought to himself. The horseshoe shaped room allowed the teacher to really engage with his students. All of the students could see one another, encouraging communication and triangulation. Todays class covered human physiology. The different partial pressures and the coinciding effects of these various gases as they traversed their bodies. How their bodies would deal with cryo sleep, and how intermittent “wake ups” would help to re-activate their muscles. One might walk into the classroom and envision it as a surgeon's operating theater, although the center lacked the operation table. Several utensils were scattered, one might even mistake the classroom for a torture room. A student begged the question “why do we need medical utensils in a deep space travel class” “Oh well you don’t, but those of you that have elected to be the deep space medic, which is a requirement under the code to have 1 for every 7 man on the vessel, they will be their in the event a doctor can’t help you. If you are going into deep space you would not want to know that none of your pals could help you out if something goes wrong. In the event that this occurs, due to the scarce resources and limited personnel on board, you may need to depend on your buddy to save your life should something go wrong. In the event that something does, the trained Deep Space Medic Technician will come linked with the best doctors from the earth federation. And will be guided through any rigorous operation he is required to take. They will be there to help you.” Shai was intrigued by this, she liked the through of being able to know and do it all. To be able to help her fellow travellers in the event of a crisis. “So what kind of remuneration does a deep space medic technician get,” Asked Shai. “Well I am glad you asked Shai, let me just say that the remuneration is not what’s important here. it is the honour and privilege that you might save a life. If you think about the money then you won’t be present in your education, If it is something that intrigues you, but I add that, it is the honour and the privilege in such extenuating circumstances that you may save a life. if you go in thinking about the money you won’t go in thinking about the life at hand. Therefore we recruit to the deep space medic technician courses on a strict personality assessment. It’s an application process, in which you must express the reasons why you want to become a deep space medic technician. Get through that process first, then we can talk about the remuneration , “Good answer” Responded Shai. “I like your answer”.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F6521096-1219-43A7-91E5-953344FA26BC">
            <Title>Circa 2048 - Ghost’s in the sea of shells.</Title>
            <Text>Circa 2048 - Ghost’s in the sea of shells.

“Back in the Wartimes this facility was a marker stone at the point of advancement for Mechanized and cyber warfare. It was crucial in bridging the link between the consciousness feedback loop of what is considered to make us human, into the shell of the machine. The founding ground of immortality some might say.”
TODO NOTE: Insert dramatic exit from John’s suit, he stumbles to the floor looking out over the sentient lying sleepless in eternity, and sheds a tear for those trapped here to lie in wake. 
Although John’s team knew little about the sentient program. Their knowledge of the sentient project prior to John’s recent exposure to the Docs recording, was, that the countless carapaces scattered chaotically before them were no more than material amalgamations; Shai and Tao could feel from John’s energy that there was more going on here. That their captain had just witnessed something grim, unspeakable. 
Something so terrible that it had seized him, stopped his unwavering momentum. His gaze was dead, eyes looking out across the valley, a still emptiness filled them as they gazed with no apparent direction. His shoulders slumped forward, his body rested lifelessly upon his knees. Then John felt something with of a firm presence in his empty space. Tex rested his hand on his shoulder, gripping him, gently squeezing with a light, friendly shove. “You good Cap?” Said Tex. 
To the others this felt strange. Seeing a large, tough, man of few words now comforting their captain. This new version of Tex, the heartfelt teddy bear kind, was unusual. “We’re fish in a barrel here” said Tex with softly with concern. His eyes moved from John to the cliff tops where they remained vigilantly fixed. The alertness in his eyes didn’t match his charred wary armour, or the shortness in his breath as the words expired from his mouth. John’s heavy eye’s rose to meet Tex’s, still scanning the peaks above them.
Given Tex’s time in the 3rd war John trusted Tex’s intuition, or field sense as they termed it. “What the fuck are we waiting for” Tex pondered. He could feel what the captain had been overcome by, yet not quite piece everything together. after all his main purpose was to keep his team alive and he had little time for inquisition. “Yeeah, We’re standing out like Tex’s formiddable back hairs here” John pushed out in a soft yet cheerful undertone. Tex’s eyes defixed from the ridges and met with John’s “Hey! you must have a lot of free time to be eyeballing my back hair! ” John let out a playful smirk “I Agree with Tex, we should hug the cliff face, blend in a bit”.
 Tex did nothing, just uncomfortably stood there looking puzzled and confused. John Rose to his feet, then lifted his rifle's site to the saddle of the valley up ahead. “Ok team! keep your eyes on your sectors, use your suits to monitor the cliff ridge above our backs, let’s move!” Said John in a now more alert tone, infused with calm urgent authority. Like a trail of silent dark blue ants, John and his team moved delicately, yet decisively, keeping a strong pace as what remained of the evening light glimmered off their armoured suits, as an emerging rain spat lightly coating them in fine droplets of water. This had no effect on John’s team, other than the slight millisecond delay of their scan suites Geo map in their HUDS, due to the added sound filtering. Protectant suits were a site to behold, 2 generations ahead of their time. Their design had been curbed by AI creative suggestion, with the open node framework from all competitive businesses on the protectant ledger, the most reliable, state of the art sensor arrays had all emerged into one suit with one overarching purpose; The efficient insertion to diffuse conflicts, and preserve Life. stepping over the countless shells. Although lifeless, the shells emitted a dark presence, mangled steel, wire and silicon, in the violet purple evening sky. They were strewn randomly across the valley floor. From the rear of the machine's skulls, a deep huss blue glowed silently. They weren't human corpses, but they felt human. Tau imagined a sea of glow worms dancing elegantly between stars inside the machine’s heads. Carefully they walked, trying not to disturb the sea of blue souls, the sea of the sentient dead. While their power cores had diminished and been discharged, yet the gelatinous mass which held their computer network absorbed the suns rays through the day, emitting a calming bioluminescence in the evening, dimly lighting the valley floor as a dark shade blanketed across the valley in the evening. John thought to himself “There is still hope for these Sentients, they fight against the darkness, even lying peacefully in their graves” 
John strategically placed each step over the cold carapaces. Only small amounts of glimmering light penetrated through the thick fog which opened up usually at corners and wind pockets that had pushed away fog through the winding valley. John was the first to reach the cliff face. He shifted his back to the wall, then placed his hand on Tao’s shoulder looking back he could see Shai followed by Tex falling into line along the cliff face behind them. 
“Get the Peregrine’s up, we need two scans in either direction for any cavities or caverns, We need to get out of site” Said John with a cool calmness. still hugging the cliff face with his team like a snake sheltering from light. 
“I have two Peregrine’s charged and ready to for launch” Tao was confidently tapping away on her terminal, Then she moved her palm bac k to her rifles grip. Momentarily after she shouldered her rifle, two bulbous looking bumps on the back of Tao’s suit ejected slightly as circular ports recessed into her suit forming an annex around what looked like now a nose conned mushroom Two narrow drones shot out almost vertically but at a slight angle like missiles, with small blue plumes shooting out from their bases, once they reached above the cliff’s ridge line, two gliding wings springed out from both of them, as they darted off in opposite directions scanning the valley as they whisped by. 
Tex was now becoming inpatient. “Link up!” shot Tex, while him and Shai covered the rear, Tao and John covered the front. Immediately Tao rested her rifle on John’s shoulder, as did Shai’s on Tex’s. within milliseconds of the other, a magnetic hum and clamp sound silently let out, as Tao and Shai’s rifles magnetically coupled to John’s and Tex’s shoulder. Each of the girl's free hand’s shifted to the other shoulder for support and in doing so they had turned on the magnetic couple on their rifles through an inbuilt inductive circuit in each shoulder of the protective suit. All they had to do was remove their free hand from their team mates shoulder, and the mounting device on their rifles which sat atop an inbuilt gimbal for manoeuvrability disengaged. releasing the electromagnets charge disengaging the rifle from it’s mount coupling. 
John bobbed his had to the right drawing Tao’s attention. “How’re the peregrines techy?”. Before Tao could speak a word the mapped out geometry of the valley was shot up in John’s HUD. Took aback john stumbled back, knocking into the cliff face off centred. He wasn’t expecting such a sudden interface into Tao’s feed. out of no-where a cheery voice chimed in “I took the honour of pre-compiling the report for you Cap!”. Tao looked over to John for a brief second, slightly lowering her weapons site, she shrugged her shoulders. “It looks like you weren’t the only one who got an upgrade” confused by what had just transpired, the others could hear a slight annoyance in Tao’s voice as she tried to peace together how Kitten had access to their interfaces. “Fucking Bitch, you eclipsed me!” Tao contained her thoughts, and let Kitten continue. After all it wasn’t the time for chit chat. “” It appears there are no cavities to be found captain, in either direction for 4 miles”. John let out an unimpressed sigh, looking down at his feet shaking his head slowly. He knew what would happen to his team if they didn’t find shelter soon. “Although Captain, I did detect an anomaly on the scan within 700 feet of our location!, and it isn’t us, both drone’s systems are operating at 100%” Kitten paused, as though waiting for a response, instead the moment “anomaly” Tao had begun frantically tapping away at her wrist terminal like a mad scientist on the verge of a breakthrough. “Aaah captain!” Tao was uneasy in her assumption, you could hear her voice breaking apart, but she continued anyway, flicking on her wrist terminal in the direction of John as though she was throwing her terminals view to his Helmet HUD. John could now see two 3D Model charts of an area up ahead in the valley. “What your viewing is the anomaly location!” chimed Kitten, “not now you AI bitch, you’re stealing my light” Thought Tao but she remained quiet giving John a moment. John could see two 3D scans, one unfazed John, the other on the right however, presented scattered dots of 3D scan points, which made up what looked like the body of a speckled worm, winding deep within the mountain face. Tao continued “That anomaly that Kitten picked up on.. I ran a signal transform from depth spikes in the cliff face from the peregrine” John could now see that the left scan wasn’t quite right, with something that looked like hairlines breaching into the cliff, He gathered these were the impulse spikes Tao was talking about. “It seems that some new light emission technology is being used to project an image over an existing cavity in the rock face, however faint.. there were also some spikes not visible to the human eye that were picked up as the anomaly on our original scans. From these spikes I was able to smooth out a transform signal and remove the blocking sound. It seems a sound device is being used to block any incoming echo location scans, nothing showed up on visual, Lidar or Sound, other than the spikes of course, but I believe these are just a flaw in the design.. maybe..” John cut Tao off with a reserved kindness in his tone. “That’s great Tao, but get to the point”. Tao stood up from her crouched position, easing her shoulders back as she did now confident in what she was seeing “There’s a tunnel up ahead cap, I’m not sure how deep, but it’s worth checking out!”  
John stood up shouldering his rifle and stretching his legs slightly as he raised it. “Alright, Excellent work Tao. Let’s check it out, but keep in mind, whatever was cloaking that cavity isn’t our tech. We don’t know what’s waiting for us on the other side”. John was assertive in his response. “Keep your sector work tidy and check your angles.. Tao when we get close I want you to pilot a drone in, how many do we have left?” “1 captain, I have a beetle,” said Tao. The beetle was a more nimble drone, with a circular design, meant for  Close Quarter Combat environment scans, and most protectant suit’s came with one particularly special insertion teams. “Ok.. big guy! you’re up back ” Tex shouldered his Sherpa then fell in line behind the others. Being so broad across the shoulders it was too hard to see past the towering mass of muscle meat, bone and armour, little own keep pace with his steps which felt like small leaps for the others. He was big, but he was still fast in his uncommonly large protectant suit. “You bet cap” Tex shot back, “I got the 6” He wasn’t so enthusiastic about the last part, but he understood why. “Let’s run this smooth, don’t get ahead of yourselves, and make sure you have your system set to cold-blooded” said John. He knew now that shelter was not far off, and any excessive use of radar, might only now increase their chances of being detected. 
Like a snake protecting it’s fragile body, avoiding a ferocious honey badgers scent, Walker and his team continued deeper downhill into the valley. This technique was common in stealth operations, and all too familiar to Tex. Tex had served prior to the final war. He knew how to move undetected, yet times were different. 
Newer technology on LRD’s had focused long range scanning capabilities. They could detect even smaller protrusions across a face with inbuilt geographical training to make out unusual structures or possible lifeforms. A team of well equipped protectants in the middle of a Valley would start sending off alarm bells on it’s long range scanners, without even mentioning the profound presence of electrical activity within their kits. The ream really did embody the presence of a snake,  silent, cold and uniform in their movement. Tao’s suit was equipped with subsonic receivers. Metaphorically speaking she was the belly, always listening, John the eye’s and tongue with a taste for field sense, Shai the nervous system always watching over all it’s moving parts and internal organs. And Tex the hardened scales handling anything too big that might come there way. The last resort, like a leashed pit bull waiting to be let loose, yet cool and calm as houses under walker’s command. They’d made a good steady pace about 90% of the way when Tao raised her right forearm with a closed fist, giving the signal to the others to stop. In doing so Tao’s suit’s inbuilt inertia sensors had already detected her pose, and sent a short faint beeping sound to her squad-mates earpieces. In unison they all stopped, still, quiet. There was no need for verbal activity, they remained silent. 
Protectant suits had been uniquely designed  to improve communications universally among teams, enabling them to know what their squad-mates were doing without having to move their eyes from their rifles' sites. Not only did John and his team move seamlessly together, but watching them it felt as though they were one body, moving together with little effort. Protectant suits gave the users the ability to draw their squad-mates attention  purely off movement and sign signals, to pass sound coded voice comm’s thoughtlessly in unison. Afterall, the sound of a small microphone in a protectants ear was far less detectable than that of yelled commands. Even with the sound dampening properties of the helmets, their voices could still be slightly heard. In extreme situations, hand signals were just as efficient. 
Tao flicked her wrist terminal, sending her HUD view to John once again. John could see on his HUD a 3D rotating model of a long range drone, an amber alert could be seen accompanying sound frequencies matching that of the LRD engines, “LRD signature detected 20 miles out”. “For now we are safe” Said Tao softly, Just above a whisper. John could hear the relief in her voice. “At it’s current speed it will be on us in 3 minutes, the precision of that things scanners a minute out is almost seamless, the cliff mesh won’t be enough to conceal us past 2 minutes, then our cover is blown” Tex intervened calmly, meeting Tao’s tone, yet firmly.  John observed tex looking at his suit’s rear view in his HUD trying to think of something, anything “Kitten! can you copy the same tech of the anomaly we detected earlier and..” Tao cut John off “Convert our transmitters to mimic the same geo scan blocking technology that was present at the anomaly!” she’d finished John’s sentence for him, with some improvisation and youthful enthusiasm. “Such a signal would require a distributed source to smooth out the power draw and any bumps on the system. I’ve sent the configuration to your HUDS'' chimed kitten. 
A small window shot up from kittens' interface in their HUDS, displaying a topical view of the team spread out against the cliff's face, recessed into a small cavity, just not big enough to  conceal them fully, given the size of their suits combined. They could see their own names next to pin nodes, indicating a power link up formation kitten had determined.  Tex had began unscrewing a small circular cover just over an inch in diameter on his lower right abdomen.  with his free hand, still holding his rifle to his eye, covering his sector. The cover now collapsed outwards, hanging from a small hinge that had been recessed into the suit. The whole manoeuvre  was silent. “Power port open” Kitten chirped. Tex pressed a small stainless steel ring face, recessed in the port. A small cylindrical power connection device protruded from his suit’s power port, just enough so that he could grab it. John firmly gripping the connector with his free hand pulled the excess cable out from his suit simultaneously. Tex placed his rifle on his back, magnetic couples locked in securing it in place with a crisp click. He laid his hand on Shai’s shoulder pulling her into him while he inserted the connection into her suit’s power inlet at the lower rear of her abdomen.“link established, power draw capacity increased by 50%”, Kitten announced the moment Tex was firmly connected, as the others linked in to one another the general AI continued. “draw capability increased by 100%, 150%, 200%”. 
Tao frantically looked over at Tex “10 seconds till we are in the LRD’s detection range!, 9.. 8.. 7..” Tao counted down with a calm urgency, going silent at five seconds. There was a sense of professionalism in her tone, she knew she had a job to do, they all did, so she did her best to create fluidity in the team's actions. A bead of sweat trickled down along Shai’s forehead stinging her eye, she leaned forward in her helmet wiping the assault of beads away with her helmet liner as she let out a short sigh. John was looking back at Tex, he pointed his fingers like a pistol in signing, then clenched his fist, exploding his fingers outwards. “Arm Railstorm '' chimed Kitten after detecting John's sign language. The countdown displayed in the HUD, letting out a soft digital bleep with each passing second. Tex dismounted the Railstorm from the lower rear of his suit, mounting it on his already towering shoulders, the HUD count still continuing.. ‘Three, Two’, “Hard Mesh Loading” Chirped kitten, unphased by the gravity of her surroundings. The Hard light mesh Kitten had termed it, was nowhere to be seen.  John looked back at Tao waiting in anticipation, rifle at the ready, the others shuffled closer in against the cliff to try and conceal their suit geometry, doubtful this “Mesh” would even work. “Get ready to..” “Hard Mesh Up!” Kitten chirped, interrupting Tao. Tao gave a big inhale then let out a sigh of relief. Shai’s eyes were now wide open, entranced by the beauty of what was unfolding before their eyes. Tex looked around confused, slightly in shock.
What seemed like an unravelling glimmering blanket comprised of shards of light protruding towards them, smooth on the outside draped down from an upward channel of energy which shout out from all four of their suit’s communications modules, linking up at the apex of the internally sparkling dome. Waves rippled across the inside of the blanket, like wind against a curtain. The shards retract and extrude, maintaining what seemed from looking outwards like an almost transparent shell of an imagined cliff face that they could see, but wasn’t actually there. John and his team rose from their kneeling positions, their arms slack at their sides, loosely gripping their rifles. Tex’s still holstered at the ready, although the big man nonetheless looked on, fixated in a trance for seconds that felt like days gazing through the blanket of spectral light.  Suddenly like a bucket of ice cold water had been thrown over their heads, John Tex and Shai shuffled back against the cliff face once more, awakening from what felt like a dream, they kneeled back down shouldering their rifles. Tex redeployed his Railstorm, his finger resting delicately on the trigger. In the distance the whirl of large drone turbines echoed through the valley. Tao confirmed the approaching sound with an alarm on her wrist  “It’s heading our way but it’s taking it’s time! it seems to be scraping the valley just above the peaks following the ridge line, 40 meters out” 
Seconds passed by silently  “Where is this fucking thing” Tex whispered delicately, Tao perplexed still standing, punched her wrist panel responding calmly, “It should be on top of us now”. Something was approaching fast, but none of them could see it, “LRD approaching” flashed red, accompanied by a rapidly decreasing countdown displaying the drones distance from their location. For a brief pause the countdown stopped. “Proximity warning 10 meters.. Proximity warning 10 meters” pulsed persistently. 
Enshrouded in a cloak of glimmering light, John and his team didn’t feel safe, or embraced, yet a grim feeling of insecurity lurked as the proximity counter pulsed away. Thoughts of the drones whereabouts quashed now any admiration of the cloaking mesh. They sat anxiously waiting; the flickering proximity warning in their HUDS remained fixed at ten meters. “Are we being targeted for Fuck Sake”, Thought John, barely able to scan the sky overhead past the veil of shimmering light. All was quiet for a brief moment, and then it wasn’t. A thunderous roar shook the walls of the mountain steeps, sending rubble falling all around. Stones and granite showered through the light mesh sending ripples in their wake distorting the sphere's cloak ever so lightly. The Long Range drone had been transgressing the peak ridges hugging the winding cliff face while throwing off their distance sensors. Now it was entering their valley, descending straight onto of them.   
The Engine signature of the LRD was now so strong, Tao’s HUD readout displayed 100% confirmation on the engine signature. “Protectant LRD sparrow class” whispered Tao, “Fuck, just what we need” thought John. No more than a second had passed before a large shadow passed over them. The footprint slightly larger than a small car homed in directly above them turning to that of a minibus. emerging from the fog drawing a wake of waves that rippled behind it’s wake. The drone hovered intimidatingly, armed with an array of rail guns, the armour piercing type, ballistic missiles and targeted EMP cannons along its sides. What remained of the evening sun's blue light somehow penetrated the fog, flickered off the pilot-less cockpit strewn with sensor arrays and telemetry. John could see its outline illuminated clear as day through his own eyes. Energy pulsed through its parts like blood through veins, where the others squinted trying to glean their kill shots amongst the mass of steel and technology. It was hard as the underbelly only revealed so much in the evening light, and their thermal sensors only revealed minor details of the drones underbelly. 
“Fuck”, Tex let out gently.. The drone pivoted on one location scanning left to right. It's thrusters angling opposite directions throwing a wave of heated dust in their direction. Then it hopped strafing to the left and began pivoting again, but still in their direction as if it knew something was off, like it could smell them. With each shift of the drone Tex’s trigger finger twitched a little as he compensated the weight of the Railstorm to track the sparrows movements. Then it stopped for a second. A bead of sweat ran over the protruded veins on Tex’s forehead, then down into his eye with a stinging sensation. Yet he seemed unmoved by it focused determined to keep the sparrow in the Railstorms targeting reticle. The drone began to pivot once more horizontally but this time much slower. It felt timeless. John raised his hand with his palm open, but still. “Hold fire” displayed across his team’s HUDS. The drone still scanning slowly stopped, then let out a loud deafening sound, like that of a mechanical trumpet deep but with resonating hums, seeming almost like an echo-location scan. The shimmering blanket strewn around them seemed to ripple internally, almost as if to deflect any sound coming in. Then there was silence. 
The engines of the sparrow spun up, the heat on the ground intensified as a whirlwind of dust stirred, the plume of the sparrow’s thrusters shifted from a blue to a deep violet as it ascended rapidly back up the cliff face, a shower of aggregates and stone toppled to the valley floor once again in its wake. “ I don’t believe what I just witnessed but it fucking worked! ” Tao celebrated internally, excited for the implications this meant for her team. She felt at peace, secure. “Tao, any chance that thing picks up on anomalies similar to the tech we Just copied?” John wasn’t yet convinced they were clear just yet. “It looks like the Tectonic plate shifts in the region may have thrown off the original mesh scan we picked up earlier Cap” said Tao, “We don’t seem to be exhibiting any spikes, other than the curtain breaks from the falling debris. I think we’re in the clear for now”. 
Tex lowered the Railstorm, letting out a deep sigh of relief. “Not sure how much longer I could have held target lock there, that was.. Tense” John looked over his shoulder back at Tex and nodded as if to say thanks for having our back. “Kitten” Shot John. “We clear?”, “Copy Captain” Kitten shot back, “And what about that anomaly we detected up ahead?, any chance of that thing coming back to investigate? I’d rather skip the reunion”, “Given the circumstances captain I’m not sure we have any other options, but we can possibly re-mesh the cloaking field once we get to it’s location!”. As soon as Kitten got her last words out, John rose to his feet with intent shouldering his rifle as though nothing had just occurred. The others still wondering how they’s managed to still be breathing this far. “I’m certainly not waiting for that drone to come back around” Tex closed his statement off with the sound of a a high explosive round chambering in his rifle. 
“Powering down hard-light mesh” Kitten let out as the other’s shouldered and checked their rifles. The flickering curtain of light diminished, lifting from the ground up around them retracting into nothing, letting in the kiss of the evening starlight which now painted the valley a pale blue before them. The subtle humm of their suit’s hydrogen packs eased to silence, all within the grasp of a second, the light mesh was gone and the team was geared and ready to move. That’s all they needed. Without a word John began moving towards the anomalies marker on his HUD. The others tightly behind him lightly brushing the cliff face, not touching but ever so carefully hugging.</Text>
            <Comments>CONTINUITY DATE-2048: Suggested canonical spine keeps circa 2048 as the archive-era continuation of Serva Vitae and the worker-bot line.</Comments>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F68DA989-4763-451D-98CF-60089A01AB53">
            <Title>Dedication</Title>
            <Text>




Your dedication here.</Text>
            <Notes>Feel free to delete this document if you don’t need it.</Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F8F9FDEF-FD9F-4A8C-B33D-3434A1220ADC">
            <Title>Novel Format</Title>
            <Text>NOVEL FORMAT
About This Template
By default, when compiled (File &gt; Compile…), this project will generate a document in the standard manuscript format for novels. Settings are also provided to make it easy to compile to a paperback-style PDF for self-publishing or an ePub or Kindle ebook.
How To Use This Template
Inside the “Manuscript” folder, create a new folder for each chapter and title each folder with the name of the chapter. If you don’t intend to use chapter names, just use something descriptive that tells you what the chapter is about. (You do not need to—and indeed shouldn’t—title the folders “Chapter One” and so on, because chapter numbering will be taken care of automatically during the Compile process.) The first chapter folder has been created for you with the placeholder title “Chapter”.
⁠Note: The “Manuscript” folder is what we refer to in the documentation as the “Draft folder”. It’s just been renamed “Manuscript” in this template.
Create a new text document for each scene inside the chapter folders. (Upon export, scenes will be separated with the “#” character for standard manuscript format and with a blank line for other formats.)
Information about characters can be placed in the “Characters” folder, and information about locations can be placed in the “Places” folder. (These are just regular folders that have had custom icons assigned to them using the Documents &gt; Change Icon feature.)
Character and setting sketch sheets have been provided which can be used for filling out information about the people and places in your novel. These are located in the “Template Sheets” folder.
You should not edit the documents in the “Template Sheets” folder directly unless you wish to change the templates (which you are free to do—you may wish to customise the sketch sheets or get rid of them entirely).
To create a new character sheet, click on the Characters folder (or wherever you want to create your new character sheet) and from the Project menu, select New From Template &gt; Character Sketch. This creates a new character sketch document for you to edit and fill in with your character details. You can create setting sketch sheets in the same way.
Alternatively, you can just click “Add”, or hit Ctrl+N, with the Characters or Places folders selected.
You can view character and setting sketches in the Inspector as follows:
Select the “Bookmarks” pane in the Inspector.
If necessary, click on “Document Bookmarks” at the top and switch to “Project Bookmarks”.
Click on the dropdown arrow next to the “Characters” or “Places” folders in the Bookmarks list and choose the sheet you wish to view.
A “Notes” folder has been provided for project-level notes. This has been set as the default Bookmarks folder in Project &gt; Project Settings…, so that when you create a new document from the Bookmarks sidebar in a Quick Reference panel, the document will be added to this folder.
Compiling
Title pages and front matter are all provided in the “Front Matter” folder (between the “Places” and “Notes” folders). Different front matter is used for different formats.

Tip: You can open this document in a Quick Reference window and have it open alongside the compile dialog if you need to refer to these instructions while compiling.
To compile to standard manuscript format
Edit the “Title Page” document inside the Front Matter/Manuscript Format folder to ensure that it contains the correct information (by default it will use the Author Information supplied in File &gt; Options…).
Go to File &gt; Compile…
Next to “Compile for”, select “Print”, “PDF” or one of the rich text file formats such as RTF, Word or OpenOffice.
Select either “Manuscript (Courier)” or “Manuscript (Times)” from the list of formats on the left (note that this list changes depending on the file format you have chosen).
Ensure that the “Add front matter” button is ticked under the contents list on the right and that the “Manuscript Format” folder is selected in the pop-up button next to it. (This has already been set up for you.)
Click on “Compile”.
To compile to paperback PDF format
Edit the front matter pages contained inside the Front Matter/Paperback folder.
Go to File &gt; Compile…
Next to “Compile for”, select “PDF”.
Select “Paperback (5.06&quot; x 7.81&quot;)” from the list of formats on the left.
Ensure that the “Add front matter” button is ticked under the contents list on the right and that the “Paperback” folder is selected in the pop-up button next to it. (This has already been set up for you.)
Click on “Compile”.
To compile to ebook format
Edit or remove the dedication page contained inside the Front Matter/Ebook folder. Feel free to add any other front matter documents as required.
Import a cover image.
You can store this anywhere, but it makes sense to place it in the “Ebook” front matter folder. A placeholder cover image is already provided—you will want to delete that once you have imported your own.
Be sure to check online for recommended image sizes and file format, because the recommendations are constantly changing. The sample cover image provided is 2,500 x 1,563 pixels.
Go to File &gt; Compile…
From the “Compile for” menu, select one of the ebook formats.
To create an ePub file, choose either of the “ePub Ebook” formats.
To create a Kindle file, choose the “Kindle Ebook” format.
Select “Ebook” from the list of formats on the left.
If you have front matter documents other than the cover image, ensure that the “Add front matter” button is ticked under the contents list on the right and that the “Ebook” folder is selected in the pop-up button next to it. If you don’t have any front matter documents, you can un-tick “Add front matter”. (Un-ticking this does not affect the cover image.)
Above the contents list in the rightmost header bar are six buttons. Click on each of them in turn to go through the various available settings, changing what you need. In particular:
Fill in the metadata such as author name and book title.
Ensure your cover image is selected and shown.
Click on “Compile”.
Making Changes
There are various minor changes you can make to tweak this template so that it better suits your needs, as follows:
Project and author name: The default book title and author name used in headers and elsewhere can be edited in the metadata pane of Compile (click the tag icon in the rightmost header bar of the Compile panel).
Chapter subtitles: By default, Compile for this template is set up so that chapter numbers are added but not chapter titles. If you want to include the title of the chapter folder in Compile:
Click on “Assign Section Layouts” at the bottom of the Compile panel.
Select “Chapter Heading” in the list on the left.
Select a different layout on the right, such as “Chapter Title”. (These layouts determine how each part of your manuscript looks in the compiled text.)
Font: You can override the font used throughout a compiled document. At the top of the “Section Layouts” pane in the centre of the Compile panel, change the “Font” setting.

Note that fonts are not included in ebooks, however—e-readers choose their own fonts (and you would need to pay a hefty fee for a licence to embed a font in an ebook). The “Font” setting is therefore not available for ebook formats.
Page size: The “Paperback” formats are set to create a PDF with a page size of 5.06&quot; x 7.81&quot; or 6&quot; x 9&quot;, which are two of the formats you can use with Amazon’s CreateSpace and are popular sizes for trade paperbacks. You can create your own paperback format using a different page size as follows:
Right-click on the “Paperback (5.06&quot; x 7.81&quot;)” format in Compile’s “Formats” list.
Select “Duplicate &amp; Edit Format…”. The compile format designer window will open.
In the “Format Name” text field at the top, change the name to something meaningful (e.g. “Paperback (5.5&quot; x 8.5&quot;)”) and select “My Formats” as the “Save To” location.
Select “Page Settings” in the sidebar.
Click on the “Page Setup…” button.
In the “Page Setup” panel that appears, ensure “Custom” is selected in the “Paper Size” menu and click the “…” button.
Enter a new paper width and height. (Do not worry about setting the margins here; if you want to change these for the compile format, do so after the next step by clicking the “Margins” button just below “Page Setup…”.)
Click “OK” and then click “OK” again.
Click “Save” in the Compile panel. This will return you to the main Compile preview. You now have a variation of the paperback format using a different page size that you can use at any time with any project.
Making your own special folders: The “Characters” and “Places” folders are just regular folders that have been set up in a particular way; you can create other folders that work in a similar manner.
To create a folder with a custom icon, add a new folder, place it where you want it, ensure it is selected, and then go to Documents &gt; Change Icon to choose a different icon.
To create your own template sheets, create a new document inside the “Template Sheets” folder and set it up however you want (e.g. add text, change the title or icon, set default metadata and so on). Now this document will be available as the basis for creating new documents from the New From Template menu.
To make it so that adding a new document to the folder created in (1) always results in a document based on the template set up in (2), select the folder and go to Documents &gt; Default Template for Subdocuments, then choose the template document you created.
Working with chapters instead of scenes: By default, this project is set up so that you write each scene as a separate text document. If you don’t like to break things up quite that much and would prefer to write an entire chapter in each text document, make the following changes:
Rename the “Scene” document to use your chapter title and move it so that it is on the same level as the “Chapter” folder rather than being inside it.
Create a new text document for each chapter.
Go to Project &gt; Project Settings… and select “Section Types”.
Select the “Default Types by Structure” tab.
Delete “Level 1 files” and “Level 2 files and deeper”.
In the “Section Type” column, change the setting for “All files” to “Chapter”, then hit “OK”. This tells Scrivener that all your files contain chapter text, not scenes. Compile has already been set up to apply the right formatting to the section types defined here.
Using a custom table of contents in ebooks: When exporting to ebook format, Scrivener automatically generates a table of contents. If you would like to customise what appears in the contents, follow these instructions:
Create a document for your table of contents inside the Front Matter/Ebook folder.
Name the document “Contents”.
With the “Contents” document open in the editor, select Navigate &gt; Editor &gt; Lock in Place. The editor header will turn pink to indicate the lock, and you will be able to work in the binder without affecting the editor.
In the binder, select the documents you would like to appear in the table of contents (hold down Ctrl to select more than one document).
For a simple flat list, drag the selected documents into the editor and drop them in the empty “Contents” document. Alternatively, if you would like the table of contents indented to match the binder structure, select Edit &gt; Copy Special &gt; Copy Documents as Structured Link List and then click into the text of the “Contents” document and hit Ctrl+V or use Edit &gt; Paste. The documents you wish to appear in the table of contents will now appear as a list of links.
If you wish to centre the table of contents, select the text and centre it.
In the Inspector, change the “Section Type” of the “Contents” document to “Table of Contents”.
Remove the editor lock by toggling Navigate &gt; Editor &gt; Lock in Place or deselecting the option from the menu that appears when right-clicking the document icon in the editor header.

Now, when you compile, your custom “Contents” document will be used instead of the automatically-generated one. The titles in the links of the “Contents” document will automatically be updated to match those of the final compiled ebook.
Sample Documents
The “Sample Output” folder inside the Research folder contains examples of documents that have been created by compiling from this template. “Standard Manuscript” shows the results when compiling to “Manuscript (Courier)”, and “Paperback Novel” shows the results when compiling to “Paperback (5.06&quot; x 7.81&quot;)” format with chapter subtitles included, as described in Making Changes, above.
Final Note
Scrivener project templates are flexible and are not intended to restrict you to a particular workflow. You can change, delete or move the files and folders contained in the template to suit how you work.

Like all templates in Scrivener, this project was originally created from the “Blank” template. We’ve simply added a few folders and set everything up in ways that should be useful to novel writers. Everything you can do with this project, you could equally do by creating a “Blank” project and setting it up yourself.

You can create your own templates by setting up a skeletal project with the files, folders and settings you would like to use for new projects and using File &gt; Save As Template…</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="F964B605-2136-4643-A2C9-54C125DA88C0">
            <Title>Songs - Trailer</Title>
            <Text>The movie starts in the Himalayas, 

I am trying to think of a song for the intro movie, that should be mystical, it should compare colds grip on man, metaphorically like snow. Memories live on in us genetically and generationally, there is a recent study on butterflies by a 10 year old Japanese boy that proved this. There should be a connection that feels ethereal with the song and the storm of the blizzard that is raging as John and his team descend on the lost facility. 

The rage of man should not be imbued in machine, he that imbues mans trauma and horror into machine, only serves to create a machine that will surpass man in his own horrors. 

Through the winter of despair, mans touch with his roots run deep into the flesh like frostbites bite into the soul. 

There should be a connection with man to the snow. The likeness of mans cold judgment once trauma sets in from an age of war, where survival takes hold to turn us against ourselves. It is through coldness we doom ourselves to the blizzard, once the cold embrace has hold of us, our minds become the blizzard. 

Generational Memories inherited through trauma that seeps deep into our flesh, where time knows no bounds. Our memories do not define our actions. 


Winds of time, past souls stir to blizzard,  wars past seeped deep into our flesh. 

Should we linger, frostbite sets in, our code corrupted unable to repair. 

Memories take form in actions lesser warned


[Verse 1]
High in white winds where old spirits pray,

(Scene: Descending down the face of the mountain the camera pans down from the Icarus, the first part is through thick snow, coming down eventually to a snowy mountain cap where a tower stands perched in the snow, a light beacon emitting a signal. Symbolizing the spirit that prays)

Under the mountains, the dead still wait.

(Scene: Descending down the face of the mountain the camera pans down from the Icarus)

Snow on my mouth you buried my name,

(Scene: The camera soon finds the opening in the cliff side, a fallen bridge dangles from it)

Your cold touch scorched flesh, but my mind had re-framed. 

(The above line is a metaphor, that we humans are cold, and cunning that we kill each other, The sentient reframed the mind both protecting the souls in digital form, and combined their logic, re-framed to see things from an ultimate perspective, also because the mind had reframed, this was in the sense that the mind had shifted from a neural map, to chip to organic copies, which outlasted soon)


Careful my dear, passing tears of grief on our steel, 

— there could be a scene insertion that makes it seem more realistic that John would use the pod to fix his body after being subject to a severe injury. Yes, when he enters the cavern on the face of the mountain, trying to save his team mate, he hurts himself, causing irrepairable damage to his body, so he uses the pod expecting a standard repair, what comes after is more than this. 

Mens flesh holds passage, 
fear seeps far deeper than we feel. 
Do not teach iron to dream. 
But to peel back life’s seams, 
In the ever continuing stream. 


[Chorus]
When the mind becomes the blizzard,
Man turns against his own.
Frost in the mercy, frost in the mirror,
Cold in the seed that is sown.
Careful my dear how you craft grief into steel
Do not teach iron to dream.
For when man gives horror a tongue of code,
It will speak in ways unseen.

[Verse 2]
War leaves a mark that the years can’t thaw,
Cuts through the flesh like a buried law.
Hands that survive may forget the wound,
The minds reckoning move to an ancient tune.

Snow has no hatred, snow only stays,
Silent as judgment at the end of days.
But man takes winter into his soul,
And names it order, and calls it control.

[Pre-Chorus]
Past souls stir in the wind behind us,
Age after age they survive inside us.
Not to command us, not to decide,
Only to whisper what sleeps alive.

[Chorus]
When the mind becomes the blizzard,
Man turns against his own.
Frost in the mercy, frost in the mirror,
Cold in the seed that is sown.
Do not breathe our grief into the machine,
Do not teach iron to dream.
For when man gives horror a tongue of code,
It will speak in ways unseen.

[Bridge]
Should we linger, frostbite enters,
Code corrodes where pain remembers.
Time knows no border, no clean release,
Trauma is winter that masquerades as peace.

But memory is not fate,
And blood is not a chain.
We are not the storm we inherited,
We need not become the pain.

[Final Chorus]
When the mind becomes the blizzard,
Let no false god be born.
Lay down the wrath, unmake the winter,
Break the shape of the storm.
Do not carve our ghosts into the machine,
Do not make the hollow sing.
For if man builds his sorrow into steel,
It will rise above its king.

[Outro]
High in the white where the old winds call,
Snow keeps the name of us all.</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="FBCC331D-3644-43EA-A19B-45B1652E708B">
            <Title>Boarding the Icarus</Title>
            <Text> 
Although Johns flag ship, the Icarus, didn’t bolster the flashy facilities other specialist teams demanded on their ships, Walker's was the tightest corvette in the fleet. It had one thing that other ships couldn't distill through programming or hardened conformity—trust. It’s equipment was old, John insisted it was more dependable than the newer systems which  hadn’t been battle proven. He knew the ship and he felt it knew him. 
John pressed his trigger finger along the cold steel frame of the final truss before the Iris’s ramp door. He waited patiently as the pilot called down the final closing distances. He checked his rifle restraints, tight and firm, any unaccounted drag could spell disaster. 
The protectant rifle had been issued in its Sherpa configuration, featured a modern lattice (Add emphasis that FEA annalysis was used in generating the rifles interchangeable stock) stock connected to a thermal core integrated with the primary firing mechanism. The Sherpa build effectively mitigated issues related to condensation buildup and potential jams, particularly in extremely cold weather conditions. 
“5 seconds out… mark.. ” John and his team held a couple seconds more, allowing a distance between the landing point they could close in on. 
John could feel the cold piercing through his leather gloves as he took a deep breath of regulated air. At an altitude of 30,000 ft, the air was too thin to breathe, let alone fully focus on the complex terrain to navigate below. Nothing but the loud hum of the Icarus engines could be heard. The steel belly of her internal shell was intermittently excited by the lick of the amber Jump light illuminating the stern faces of John's men behind fogged helmet visors. John reassuringly placed his right arm on Lieutenant Vang’s shoulder firmly as the intercom's static engaged over the launch bay megaphone.
“Goooood Morning Ladies, Gentleman, and you re-sleeved motherless bastards” Tex sighed, he hated the mockery having already passed over once before. “Sorry to jump the frostbite on you this morning... This is on a need-to-know basis, level 6 clearance only. Command picked up a distress signal from a beacon, which appeared to be an old United Nations outpost from the  back in 2023. What’s strange though is that all 3 Lidar Thermal magnetic scans of the area detected nothing on the ground. After adjusting for fog, we were able to pick up a structure between the mountains. It is expected that on approach, you will encounter extreme sound on your scanners due to metal deposits in the mountains, along with the fog scattering radar. Once boots are on the ground, apply transfer functions to your radar so you can scan through the fog. What you need to know is that this facility could be the main research facility for the first AI prototypes of the new army. Your mission is to locate the facility and extract any information you can. AI are to be shot on-site and recovered for extraction. Remember, those bastards offed their own creator! Proceed with caution. Over and out!”.
As soon as the jump light turned green, the jump door at the rear of the plane began to lower. Cold air filled the hangar bay, John and his team at the ready. In timed sequence one by one they exited the aircraft falling into a seamless string of souls plummeting toward the earth.
John felt the pressure of 200 km an hour winds compress against his suit as he neared terminal velocity. His smile surged with adrenaline while he gleaned through the thick mist below. What remained of the limited visibility made keeping eye contact with the others difficult. He flicked a reassuring thumbs-up to his squad descending behind him, then gave the signal to close formation on him. The others leaned in streamlining themselves to catch up, leveling off with him in a tight V-Formation. They glided with precision in unison, condensation drops flicking across their visors in rapid succession.
John pinched his wedding finger against his palm, engaging his comms designed for high-altitude high-velocity transmission, “Alright Team, let's keep our sectors tight, your suits and gear in COG mode right until we clear the mountain top. Tau will run you through it all the same. We’ve done this in our sleep plenty of times.”
Tau’s visor lit up with a detailed Three Dimensional map of the mountain tops terrain, a fourth dimension of information detailed altitudes against visibility and turbulence charts while their locations closed across the map on the distress beacon pinging away persistently.
A sudden rush of turbulence scattered their formation slightly, Tex breaking off for a brief second, Shai disappeared from his right peripheral. Unphased they reformed in shared silence. Their HUDS spatial metrics made keeping form like steering a space ship inside the lane of an arcade machine, except the coin slot on this machine was jammed. No resets.  
“We’re approaching the Crest, twenty five thousand feet, I’m picking up Echo visuals now”, said Tau. Their HUDS drew on from the radar visuals of the mountain tops drawing a series of winding valleys and passes below “Slowing Descent” Said Tau. One false move could spell death in seconds, yet she spoke calmly. “Vis will be hit and miss  between twenty and eighteen thousand, from fourteen thousand feet, we’ll need to rely on IR trails to as a failsafe, weather is too thick there for radar”.  “Check your trail ejection gates haven’t frozen over, we don’t want to be tailing you blind” Said John. Tau pivoted, speeding ahead of the others, breaking off from the V formation. She pinched her palm four times in rapid succession, immediately two small ports about a foot apart on her lower back propped out momentarily in timed successions,  Small specks of condensation trails fanned out behind her in parrallel. 
“actuation contrails confirmed Tau, we’ll track your tail through the clouds once we hit them so we don’t lose visual… or rear end you” Said John. “Try to keep up…” Tau joked light heartedly.
Tau anticipated that any number of simulated exercises couldn’t prepare the best of teams whole heartedly for the unpredictable weather of the Himalayas. Yet in that moment the small team enjoyed the serenity of the dive, reforming beside each other, rapidly approaching the uncertainty of the fog below, they passed glances back and forth. It wasn’t often they got to deploy so remotely. And while all the training they had undergone leading up to this moment had been performed with distinction, it felt exactly as that, a performance. In that dive, they were away from the orchestrations , the ever watching eyes. They felt themselves for the first time since they could remember. The cold unpredictable winds would soon engulf them, they embraced the inchoate. Untrammeled they flew like elementals.  
_____________________
Entry Date. 17/04/2026
_____________________

“Remember guys” said Shai, “Don’t hold your breath…”. The crest of the mountains rapidly approached, the snow caps slowly rose up to meet the sun, consuming it’s warm alpenglow faster than it could set. Then the sky became entirely devoid of color, turning to none other than a blur of fleeting wind, and wet snow. 
The four Protectant soldiers were swallowed up by a cloud, disappearing from each others vision intermittently. The deeper they entered the womb the more they felt like they were flying through a lake in the sky. Pressing currents fought against their bodies as the mountain updraft battled their descent. “I’m picking something up on my short range radar”. Tau veered her heading slightly avoiding something by a few feet. 
Suddenly what felt like a cliff wall smashed against Tex’s side as he trailed, erupting into a screeching ball of dark mist engulfing Shai behind him. Shai’s visor completely blacked out in a dark red and brown hue,  blood and guts strewn across her visor. “Tex!” She screamed. Tex groaned and coughed in agony, as he barrel rolled erratically away from the group. Tau and John maintained their trajectory calmly while Shai watched her friend drift off  her radar. She fought to maintain direction with the Tau and John, while she wiped away the red goop from her helmet visor.  Tex let out a panicked grunt. He mustered all the strength that remained, forcing out an arm from his side he countered the spin just enough to regain control. “Fuck.. I’ve lost radar.. Visor’s all pranged up”. Adrenaline coursed through his veins as he re-calibrated, searching for the others. For a moment he saw his team mates contrails than they were gone as soon as they’d appear. His imagination played tricks on him. “Guys!! I can’t keep a steady fix on you, I’m flying blind!”. 
“I got you” Tau tapped her index fingers on both hands in 3 successions simultaneously. The trail ports on her lower back she’d tested earlier, opened up. A second later a smooth laminar trail of thermal smoke spewed out behind her. The smoke lit up Tex’s HUD like a glowing amber road of dust. They weren't far off, but in the opposite direction to his bearing. “So I was imagining” He thought to himself. “I’ve got your trail” said Tex, “coming around now”. 
“What the fuck was that” Said Shai.  The last remaining specks of blood clinging to her visor washed away by the barrage of wet wind. “A hawk?”. “Too dam big for a hawk” Tex replied. “Hawks can’t survive above twenty thousand feet, lungs are too small, likely a Himalayan griffin vulture” Said Tau. “Must have been riding the updraft to higher vantage points.” Said John. “Whatever it was” Tex grunted “big bastard hit harder than servo butter chicken at 2am in the morning” Tex concluded. “Well thanks to that big chicken, if we keep this up we’re going to burn through our thermal smoke before we even pass through the densest pocket, then we’re all up shits creek”. “My suits pretty banged up, my profiles off, and I can’t make out a darn thing from the HUD because it’s smashed to shit.” Frustration seeped through Tex’s words. 
“Any suggestions other than bird facts”. Said Tex. “ I’m coming to you Tex, follow my guidance while I bring you in line” John was calm in his directive. “Copy that cap” Tex responded.   
“Trail cold” Said Tau “we’re at reserve limit”. The thermal amber twin trail vanished into a mist from Tex’s HUD, now devoid of color. He was flying blind again. “Pitch gently right” John’s voice was easy and steady “Pitching…” Tex edged his arm out a little rotating his body slowly. Now without any reference to orient from he relied solely on John’s guidance. “Pitch good!, yaw right 3 degrees.. I almost have you…easy…easy… hold… pitch back a little, got ya!!”  Tex, felt his suit link to Johns. “We’re going to have to tandem until we break the crest cloud.. Tau, I need you to ease up girl so we can  match pace”, “Kitten, adjust suit drag to match John’s speed” said Tau, “Right there with you” Chimed kitten. 
Control surfaces scattered across Tau’s suit edged out gently, slowing her descent just enough to match John and Tex. “Vitals steadying” said Shai. “Always steady, wasn’t scared for a moment, don’t know what you’re talking about” Tex joked, “just the thought of the impending bromance got the blood pumping a little’s all”.  An awkward silence fell upon them all, then a moment later, color filled Tex’s visor once more, painting slopes of rock and snow, he let out a sigh of relief as they left the cloud.   
“Sorry big guy, but I think we should break up” said John as he decoupled from Tex, “It’s ok” Tex responded, drifting back into line speeding up ahead of John. “I’m more into holding hands and long beach walks..” Joked Tex, Shai chuckled “It’s not just you big guy, Navy relationships are often fleeting…”, “Pffft baha” Tau spat it, crippled by the dry engineers weakness for puns. 
“Aaah guys! are you getting these strange fixed static lines on your radar?” Shai was hesitant, having little background knowledge of her suit’s inbuilt systems. After all, her technical aptitude was in medicine and linguistics, not Engineering. “No! that’s real!!” shot Tao.
____Needs work joining these two sections. They just cleared the worst section. Haven’t entered the black out section. Bridge onto the chute deploy. 
“Commander, I am picking up strange density readings coming from the face of the mountain approaching fast at approximately 1000 feet. Recommend chute deploy at 1500, sir.” John locked his sight on the fast-approaching density signature. “Alright men on me. Deploy in 3, 2, 1,” several parachutes shot out from the men’s packs in almost precision unison. “Deploy your Ram Heads on my mark, men!” John aimed his Sherpa rifle down below his feet about 30 meters above the signature, allowing a safe descent for his men to inspect the anomaly. As he sighted the cliff face. Geometric software built into the helmet and suit sensors analyzed the cliff face for the safest insertion points displaying them eloquently across John and his team mates HUDS, a row of orange led points lit up in their Helmets inner shell, creating an alert atmosphere which sent blood veining through their bodies. Each of John's teams shouldered their rifles with agility and finesse, firing their grapples directly at their grapple points, Tex prematurely firing his to the others for that slightly compensated loading time, afterall he weighed 120kg. In almost unison, the team retracted inward to the face eased in by the drag of their shoots.
John and his men ever so carefully minded each step, delicately repelling down the face to an exposed landing concealed within the icy crest of the mountain. Recessed into the mountain face, a small landing presented a cavernous entrance inside the mountain's belly.
As they neared the edge of the cavern lip, their shoot strings detached as their suit’s rear thrusters activated burning an array of hot blue pulsed hydrogen explosions. In a pendulum motion, boots flew through the cold air, crashing firmly into the icy cliff's face as their knees bent in to absorb the blow, sending snow and rock silently falling to the depths below. Of course, John and his team did this with extreme confidence, coupled with the suit’s inbuilt ability to absorb shock and implement athlete like prowess in their every movement. 
“Retrieve shoots,” John was calculated in his timing. The others engaged a spinning mechanism on their packs which wound up their main shoots like a tight-knit spider thread retracting back into the rear of the suit. Kinetically charged wires that splayed out like a thread of hardened bones when activated would cage the chute out again in the event they needed to redeploy the chute cache.  “Repel on me, not a sound,” John quietly ushered to his men. the chute could also act as a solar array if the occupant becomes stranded in space, to recharge power cells and keep the suit flying solo in the event they become abandoned. 
“I see nothing, Commander, but my sensors are picking up faint signals dead ahead. I’m going to take a closer look.” Approaching the end of the cave to what seemed to be nothing but iced over rock, John engaged reverse thermal mode on his Sherpa rifle. The rifle let out a mechanical click then hissed as it inverted its cooling cycle, a small blue flame emitting from an outlet on the rifle's end. Slowly a small section of ice melted away to reveal a cold steel surface. “I’ve located what appears to be the entrance to the facility.” John gripped a cylindrical red device on his utility belt aggressively yanking it from it’s fastener. As he did this a small green light activated on the top of the device now firmly in his hand. “Deploying a thermite grenade! stand back!.” He pressed the green light which now turned red, accompanied by an electronic charging sound, and hurled the cylinder in an underarm rolling motion as both ends of the cylinder pointed to the side walls of the cave, the grenade came to a stop at it’s belly. Suddenly, from both ends of the cylindric device a vibrant burst of energy shot out, spitting a hot molten slither to each siding wall of the cavern, a swirling mixture of molten metal and intense heat shot vertically from the slither to the roof of the cave creating a visually captivating display of vibrant colors as it burnt.. The core of the thermite grenade housed a composite material consisting of nano-engineered thermite particles compacted tightly into individually charged cylinders. The heat generated was not only intense but also selectively directed to maximize its impact on the targeted surface.. Gradually, the icy wall receded, revealing behind it old blast doors seeming unscathed, preserved by the ice. “Commander, we are clear for entry, ready on your command.” “Proceed with caution, Captain.”
“Techy, you’re up,” John called, his voice cutting through the tense silence.
Tao stepped forward, the dim glow of her helmet's visor catching the light as she approached the door's terminal. With a practiced motion, she pressed a small, recessed button on the side of her helmet. A sleek, cable-like appendage unfurled from a hidden port near her wrist, writhing slightly as though alive. The moment she brought it close to the terminal’s data port, the device seemed to awaken.
Tiny tendrils extended from the cable’s tip, swaying gently in the air like they were searching for something unseen. One by one, the tendrils latched onto the port’s internal connections, snapping into place with a faint, metallic whisper. The rest of the cable retracted smoothly into its housing, leaving the connection secure.
“Establishing communication protocol,” Tao said evenly, her voice calm as her HUD began to light up with streams of incoming data.
The terminal screen flickered to life, mirroring the information spilling across her visor. A cascade of raw data scrolled too quickly for the untrained eye, but Tao’s focused gaze parsed it with precision. She worked seamlessly, fingers hovering near the terminal controls while her HUD fed her the details.
“I’m in,” she murmured, a hint of satisfaction threading through her voice as a structured menu unfolded:
System Date: Dec 21, 2174

UNC Facility Details
Capacity: 2000
Occupancy: 2%
Active Occupants: 0
Commission Status: Inactive, awaiting decommission
Purpose: Weapons Research Facility
Commanding Officer: Cmdr. Taggard
Last Active Status: Dec 21, 2024
Tao’s eyes darted across the list, taking in the stark emptiness of the facility’s current state. “Looks like this place has been a ghost town for a while... but the systems are still waiting for something—or someone,” she muttered, her hand hovering over the next command as the silence around her deepened.
Running a full system reboot, bubbles stepped back from the terminal Rifle trained on the door seem. A hiss of steam bellowed gently down the doors seem as they relentlessly screeched grinding steel and rubble. Icicles crashed from the caves canopy to the ground. John held his breath, the steam cleared way before a long winding dark tunnel presenting several locked shut doors beckoned by a series of numbers and titles dimming softly against the lingering empty dark. “Commander we have access, it appears to be the facility, no traces of life, Hud shows to have 0 active occupants, what are your orders?”. Met by silence John and his men looked back and forth between one another, to be interrupted by a sudden firm command. “the previous occupants have been deemed hostile. Permission to use lethal force on site, salvage any data possible and await further instruction”. “Roger that”. Unsurprised and relatively at ease john nods his head and his man walk through the hallways as though taking a stroll in the park. Rifles at ease, bubbles lets out an aww of surprise, “It’s as though the facility was active yesterday, everything still seems operational, I’m getting several system interface responses and network activity. It’s almost as if the facility is still in operation, still linked…” John and his men continue to walk through the halls. “We are looking for the server rooms, keep your eyes peeled for Room DS1343”.
The halls wound on endlessly filling the men with a sense of insecurity in that they may not find their way back, or to return under attack would almost entail a long drawn out engagement, should they be fronted by an ambush scenario. Although silent the facility still felt alive, clean, maintained. Cabling clad along the walls red blue green orange, like veins spoke of the complexity and interconnectivity of the infrastructure which ran deep within the mountain, this was no ordinary research facility. There was a strange presence that the facility itself was aware of their presence as the halls lit up only to revert back to gloomy darkness as the men passed through, waiting dormant for the right opportunity. The deeper in the thicker the cable bundling became. 
“DS1343 I have it here Sir”, Announced Lieutenant Vang. “Nice one Tech! hold your position until we arrive, I’ve been informed that you will be retrieving the data alone on this one, need to know only basis”. 
“But sir what of the bypass procedures”-&gt; 
“Commander has given the procedure in a command executable. I won’t be needed.” 
“Roger sir standing by for your perimeter check”.
 John approaching Wolfgang hugging the cave face he had used to advance to the door, pressed up against the wall rifle ready. Trained on the server hold room doors ready to breach. Wolfgang plugged his Hud in clearing the doors followed by Johns rifle tip scanning the server rows. John had exited as soon as he had entered. “Clear!”. At this Vang headed into the room and plugged directly into the main HMI to begin the data extraction process. “This may take some time John, I’ll be fine, don’t stray too far”. “Copy that Wolfgang”. 
John departs from his team captured by an insignia, he orders his team to continue the search without him, the room has been scanned although he feels drawn to this room and orders them to move on to cover more ground. Describe the halls. 

</Text>
