Design Note: Atmospheric Trail Ejection System (ATES)
System Overview
A suit-mounted pyrotechnic/chemical deployment system designed for high-altitude, low-visibility team tracking (e.g., skydiving through high-density snow/clouds).
1. Component Placement: Lower Lumbar / Rear Hip
Location: Dual ports located on the lower back, just above the glutes, angled slightly outward.
Rationale: Placing ports on the &quot;lee side&quot; (back) of the diver protects the mechanisms from direct ram-air cooling and moisture impact at terminal velocity. This position utilizes the body’s natural aerodynamic &quot;teardrop&quot; to seed the smoke directly into the stable vortices trailing the legs.
Visual Result: Creates two clean, parallel &quot;braiding&quot; trails that avoid the turbulent &quot;burble&quot; behind the head and shoulders, ensuring the diver’s own vision remains clear.
2. Redundancy &amp; Navigation: Dual-Port Configuration
Depth Perception: Two parallel lines allow followers to judge distance and closure rates (stereoscopic tracking).
HUD Integration: Dual heat/density signatures allow the &quot;Echo&quot; HUD to calculate the lead diver’s roll and bank angle in real-time.
The &quot;Corridor&quot;: The gap between trails marks the low-drag wake, allowing the team to &quot;slot in&quot; and maintain high speeds with less effort.
3. Cold-Weather Reliability: Intermittent Actuation
The &quot;Freeze&quot; Risk: High-moisture environments (snow clouds) combined with sub-zero temps cause &quot;ice bridging&quot; on ejection gates.
Operational Protocol: &quot;Pulse&quot; or intermittent firing. This cycles the mechanical gates to break up ice and uses the chemical reaction’s high thermal output to flash-evaporate moisture and keep the &quot;ejection gates&quot; clear.
4. Sensor Visibility: Thermal vs. Radar
Thermal/IR: The primary tracking method. The smoke is a byproduct of a high-heat reaction, making it &quot;glow&quot; on thermal HUDs against the freezing atmospheric backdrop.
Radar (Echo): The density of the aerosolized trail provides a consistent backscatter, allowing the HUD to draw &quot;Echo visuals&quot; (the misty road) even when visual sight (Vis) is zero.
5. Atmospheric Behaviour
Cold Air Effect: The dense, freezing air keeps the trails &quot;tight&quot; and low-hanging, preventing rapid dissipation.
Vortex Seeding: At high speeds, the trails will naturally swirl into tubes, making them look like &quot;fine misty roads&quot; or &quot;white snakes&quot; in the sky.</Comments>
